\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED - CORRECTED-UPDATE 1-Japan reserves hit record high, China catchin  \b0\par\par\uc2 554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2006\par 05:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  In TOKYO story headlined "Japan reserves hit record high,   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China catching up" please read in 10th paragraph ...\par October-December quarter ... instead of ... September-December\par quarter ... (corrects period).\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2  A corrected version follows.  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 7 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves crept up to a record high in January but looked set to be overtaken by China's rapidly swelling pool as the world's largest in a matter of months.  \par \par \uc2  Japan's external reserves rose to $851.666 billion by the end of January, up from $846.897 billion a month earlier and the previous record of $847.766 billion set in August, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2  The euro's rise against the dollar bolstered the value in dollar terms, but an absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  meant \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves would be little changed -- unlike China's, which have been rapidly expanding as Beijing fights persistent upward pressure on the yuan under its "managed float" regime.  \par \par \uc2  "China is letting the yuan rise in very small increments, so the need to buy dollars remains. So long as it continues this policy, the pace of buying will remain constant, and its reserves will keep rising," said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan Chase Bank.  \par \par \uc2  "It's just a matter of time before China's reserves overtake \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's, particularly given that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment."  When \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trying prevent a soaring yen from derailing its nascent export-led recovery a few years ago, it bought a record 20 trillion yen ($168 billion) worth of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- predominantly dollars -- for yen in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen worth in the first three months of 2004.  \par \par \uc2  But since then, it has refrained from making forays into the market, not least because the yen has been declining on its own accord, thanks to rock-bottom interest rates that contrast with rising U.S. rates.  \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at around 119 yen <JPY=> on Tuesday, compared with around 103.50 yen, its lowest level in about four years, hit in March 2004 when Japan last interevened. Dollar/yen trading was at 102.50 yen at the start of 2005.  \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by China in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to contain yuan strength helped to push its official reserves to $818.9 billion at the end of 2005.  \par \par \uc2  Given that Chinese reserves jumped around $50 billion in the October-December quarter, its reserves are widely expected to eclipse Japan's during the first quarter of the calendar year.  \par \par \uc2  Beijing's reserves have swelled in recent years as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  absorbed foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  generated by China's growing trade surplus and inflows from foreign direct investment as well as speculative capital.  \par \par \uc2  Beijing revalued the yuan by 2.1 percent against the dollar last July and pledged to allow more freedom in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's movement under a so-called managed float exchange rate regime.  \par \par \uc2  Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has limited the yuan's cumulative appreciation to less than 0.7 percent since the July revaluation.  ($1=118.98 Yen)     \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-JAPAN-RESERVES (UPDATE 1, CORRECTED)|LANGEN|Table|J|ABN|M|D|NAT|Z|E|RBN|U|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060207e2270003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
