\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  cuts yen's global role-Fed's Olson  \b0\par\par\uc2 403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2006\par 02:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Updates with background, additional comments, paragraphs 4 to end)  \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Jan 23 (Reuters) - U.S. Federal Reserve Board Governor Mark Olson said on Monday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one factor that has diminished the yen's global role in comparison with the U.S. dollar and the euro.  \par \par \uc2  Speaking to an audience at Susquehanna University in Selinsgrove, Pa., Olson said he thought there were a number of reasons the yen did not circulate as broadly outside Japan as the dollar and the euro do outside their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  areas.  \par \par \uc2  "Number one, I think is that it lacks some stability. Number two, the Japanese are very much involved with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at times ... it is subject to a lot more potential volatility. And third you don't have the liquidity in the market," he said in answer to a question from the audience. An audio feed of his remarks was monitored in Washington.  \par \par \uc2  Tokyo spent a record 35 trillion yen in 2003 and the first three months of 2004 combined to stem the yen's rise. It has stayed out of the market since March 2004.  \par \par \uc2  Asked about the inversion of the U.S. yield curve, Olson said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not in the business of trying to determine the shape of the U.S. yield curve, but that it was important to understand the factors behind market yields.  \par \par \uc2  "Whatever is happening with respect to the (U.S.) yield curve. it is an international phenomenon," Olson said, adding that "you would find exactly the same line" in looking at yields in Germany, Britain and Japan.  \par \par \uc2  "What we try to do is understand the reason, but we don't try to determine what it should appropriately be," he said.  \par \par \uc2  Before taking questions, Olson spoke broadly on issues relating to productivity, globalization and inflation, but steered clear of commenting directly on the outlook for the U.S. economy and interest rates.  \par \par \uc2  As he had in similar remarks on Jan. 11, he said an expanding global workforce as Russia, China and other countries turned toward free markets had helped dampen wage rates around the globe and lowered inflation pressures. He said, however, this "seminal" shift eventually would run its course.      \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-FED-OLSON (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|U|D|J|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060124e21o0002l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
