\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ INTERVIEW: Dlr/Yen May Rise, Only Temporarily-Nikko Citi  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 2006\par 07:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar may rebound against the yen over the next few months, but it will stay on course to end this year at Y100.00 - or even below - as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lose support from interest rate advantages, said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Nikko Citigroup Limited.  \par \par \uc2 Over the past several weeks, the dollar has fallen against the yen on growing speculation that the U.S. Federal Reserve is about to end its campaign of raising interest rates, and many traders expect the greenback to trend lower this year. But with Japanese money continuing to flow into overseas assets, the dollar could rise to Y116.00 over the next few months, Masafumi Yamamoto told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview. Around 0250 GMT Tuesday, the dollar was at Y114.94.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese investors are expected to continue buying overseas assets paying higher interest, and at the same time hedge funds and other speculators are likely to sell the yen in anticipation of such Japanese moves, said Yamamoto, who worked at the Bank of Japan as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer and international financial analyst for 10 years before joining Nikko Citigroup in November.  \par \par \uc2 'We're expecting the yen to remain weak between January and March' against the greenback, he said.  \par \par \uc2 But after that quarter, Yamamoto said the dollar will probably fall to Y109.00 to Y108.00 in the April-June period. From then to the end of year it could decline to Y100.00, or to even Y98.00, he added.  \par \par \uc2 'The rate gaps between the U.S. and other countries will gradually narrow' to the dollar's disadvantage in the coming months, he said.  \par \par \uc2 'With the U.S. economy expected to slow down this year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets' focus will then probably return to the large size of U.S. current account deficit' from interest rate differentials, he said. 'And the dollar will weaken broadly.'  \par \par \uc2 Once such broad dollar selling gains steam, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be able to hold firm against the yen, even if Japanese investors keep buying overseas assets and the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues its zero interest-rate policy, he said.  \par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to sell yen if the dollar-yen rate falls at a rapid pace, Yamamoto said such a move may not have a substantial impact.  \par \par \uc2 'It would be difficult (for the ministry) to turn around the overall trend' of dollar weakness once it begins, he said. He added that unless Japan's economy slips into a severe downturn this year, the ministry may stay on the sidelines or, even if it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may only try to slow the pace of dollar declines.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar Will Fall Once Rate Gaps Begins To Narrow  \par \par \uc2 The imbalance in the U.S. balance of payments has up to now been overshadowed by continued, stair-step rate hikes by the Fed. But Yamamoto said the situation will change after the Fed's policy meeting scheduled for Jan. 31, in which he expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates to 4.50% from 4.25% but at the same time will end its credit-tightening campaign. The Fed may even start cutting rates late this year if the U.S. economy slows with the country's housing markets and capital investments, he said.  \par \par \uc2 In contrast, central banks in other advanced economies including the euro zone, Canada and Switzerland are expected to push up interest rates gradually, he said. For example, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to lift its interest rate to 2.50% from 2.25% during the second quarter and then to 2.75% in the third quarter, Yamamoto said. The Bank of Japan, however, is likely to hold rates near zero throughout 2006, he said.  \par \par \uc2 A recent pattern of money flows suggests that a weakening in foreign purchases of U.S. bonds would be bad news for the dollar, he said.  \par \par \uc2 'There has been little direct investment coming into the U.S.,' while there has been continued direct investment flowing overseas from the U.S., Yamamoto said. 'Inflows into U.S. stocks have also been lackluster compared with those into the equities markets of other countries.'  \par \par \uc2 Another problem facing the dollar is the possibility that U.S. President George W. Bush's administration may try to lower the dollar's value to please U.S. manufacturers as U.S. statewide elections in November draw near, he said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Shawn Schroter  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060117e21h003xp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
