\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  says did not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market in Dec  \b0\par\par\uc2 237 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2005\par 11:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 30 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not conduct any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between Nov. 29 and Dec 28, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 It was the 21st straight month that Japan has stayed out of the market, the longest stretch since the MOF began keeping such records in 1991.  \par \par \uc2 The last time Japanese authorities sold yen to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was on March 16, 2004, when it was trading at about 108.60 to the dollar <JPY=>.  \par \par \uc2 Japan sold 35 trillion yen ($298 billion) in the 15 months to March 2004 to weaken the yen, due to concerns that excessive yen strength could weaken demand for Japanese exports and hurt the nation's economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 But the economy has since been improving gradually and Japan is seen snapping out of a near eight-year bout of deflation.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese consumer prices rose for the first time in two years in November.  \par \par \uc2 The yen was trading around 117.30 yen on Friday, having tumbled more than 12.5 percent against the dollar since the beginning of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Yen weakness seems to be less of a concern to the Japanese government than yen strength as officials have for the most part kept quiet about the yen's fall.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|U|M|D|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051230e1cu000ek\par }\page {\par\fs20