\par\par \b \uc2 Summary-Japan's FY06/07 budget totals Y79.69 trln yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 938 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2005\par 01:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 20 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) unveiled a blueprint on Tuesday of the government's budget for the fiscal year from next April.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF's blueprint of the budget will form the basis of the government's draft budget, which is to be approved by the cabinet on Dec. 24 after some last-minute negotiations this week and submitted to parliament in January.  \par \par \uc2 Following are a summary and a table, based on figures provided by the ministry.  \par \par \uc2 BUDGET SIZE  \par \par \uc2 -- The size of the 2006/07 initial budget will be around 79.69 trillion yen, down 3.0 percent from this year's 82.18 trillion yen initial budget. That would be the first time in four years that the size of the initial budget is smaller than the previous year's and the first time since 1998/99 that it is less than 80 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 -- General spending will fall to 46.37 trillion yen, down 1.9 percent from 47.28 trillion yen this year, the second straight year of decline.  \par \par \uc2 -- Most of the rest of the budget goes to debt servicing costs, which will total 18.76 trillion yen, and to regional spending totalling 14.56 trillion yen. The ministry assumed a 2 percent yield on 10-year Japanese government bonds when calculating debt servicing costs.  \par \par \uc2 -- Tax revenues are estimated at 45.88 trillion yen, up from this year's forecast of 44.01 trillion yen. This year's figure is expected to be revised up to around 47 trillion yen due to higher-than-expected corporate profits. Tax revenues are recovering thanks to an economic recovery, but are still short of the more than 50 trillion yen in tax revenues seen annually in the 1990s.  \par \par \uc2 -- Official development assistance (ODA) for poor countries is set to be cut by 3.4 percent from this year's budget to 759.7 billion yen.  \par \par \uc2 -- Defence-related spending is set to be cut 0.9 percent to 4.81 trillion yen. Spending to support the presence of U.S. forces in Japan will fall by 7.4 percent to 215.1 billion yen.  \par \par \uc2 -- Social welfare costs will rise 0.9 percent to 20.57 trillion yen despite government efforts to reform the nation's medical care system to make patients to pay more for their health care.  \par \par \uc2 BOND ISSUANCE  \par \par \uc2 -- Fresh issuance of Japanese government bonds (JGBs) is planned at 29.97 trillion yen, down 12.8 percent from the initial 34.39 trillion yen issuance earmarked for this year -- the largest drop from year-ago levels on record.  \par \par \uc2 -- It would also be the first time in five years the government has kept the number below 30 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 -- Japan will rely on bonds for 37.6 percent of its overall budget in fiscal 2006/07, down from 41.8 percent this year.  \par \par \uc2 -- Japan's outstanding public debt, including those held by central and local governments, is expected to rise to 775 trillion yen at the end of fiscal 2006/07, roughly 151 percent of the nation's gross domestic product.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -- The government's key fiscal consolidation target is to\par move the primary balance -- revenues and spending excluding debt\par issuance and servicing -- into surplus by the early 2010s. The\par balance will be in deficit by 11.2 trillion yen in fiscal\par 2006/07, improving from a 16 trillion yen deficit in this fiscal\par year.\par  -- A special account that holds \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 assets acquired through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , called the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Fund Special Account (FEFSA), will continue transferring surplus funds to the general account. For the next fiscal year, 1.62 trillion yen will be transferred from the FEFSA to help support the revenue of the general account budget, up from 1.42 trillion yen this fiscal year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EXTRA BUDGET  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The government is to adopt a 4.52 trillion yen extra budget for the current fiscal year to next March, in part to fund steps to deal with natural disasters, asbestos and bird flu. Additional tax revenue of around 3 trillion yen this fiscal year and surplus funds from the previous fiscal year will help finance the extra budget. They will also help keep new JGB issuance in fiscal 2005/06 to 33.5 trillion yen, down from an initial estimate of 34.4 trillion yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FILP  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The Fiscal Investment and Loan Programme (FILP), which uses funds from public financial activities and is often referred to as "the second budget", is projected at 15.00 trillion yen, down 12.5 percent from 17.15 trillion yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BUDGET BREAKDOWN  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Figures for initial budgets for fiscal 2005/06 and 2006/07 are in billions of yen, with percentage changes in parentheses:  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                              FY06/07   FY05/06   Change\par  TOTAL                         79,686.0   82,182.9   (-3.0)\par  REVENUES\par   Tax and stamp revenues       45,878.0   44,007.0   (+4.3)\par   Non-tax revenues              3,835.0    3,785.9   (+1.3)\par   Government bonds             29,973.0   34,390.0  (-12.8)\par  SPENDING\par   General Expenditures         46,366.0   47,282.9   (-1.9)\par  Social security            20,570.1   20,380.8   (+0.9)\par  Public works                7,201.5    7,531.0   (-4.4)\par  Defence                     4,813.7    4,856.4   (-0.9)\par  ODA                           759.7      786.2   (-3.4)\par  Government debt servicing     18,761.6   18,442.2   (+1.7)\par  Tax grants to local govts     14,558.4   16,088.9   (-9.5)\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-JAPAN-SUMMARY|LANGEN|Table|J|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|NAT|U|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051220e1ck0000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
