\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen darts to 6-week high vs dollar   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano   \par \uc2 793 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2005\par 07:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 16 (Reuters) - The yen shot to a six-week high against the dollar and a one-month peak versus the euro on Friday, extending its rapid rally this week as yet more traders bought back the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a year-long slide.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said that buying back of the yen was inevitable after the market had extended bets against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to record number of contracts earlier in the month.   \par \par \uc2  Speculators, such as hedge funds, were also seen unwinding positions after borrowing the yen for almost no cost and selling it in carry trades to buy higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the U.S., Australian and New Zealand dollars.   \par \par \uc2  "The market is testing how far this unwinding of carry trades will go," said Nobuo Ibaraki, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. "It will take about one to two weeks to find a bottom."   \par \par \uc2  The yen's huge turnaround this week has prompted officials from Japan's Finance Ministry to sound warnings they are keeping a close eye on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  But most traders say that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market at the yen's current level against the dollar, still more than 10 percent cheaper than this year's peak scaled in January.   \par \par \uc2  Speculators on the International Monetary Market in Chicago had extended shorts on yen futures to a record 71,643 contracts in the first week of December.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell as far as 115.61 yen <JPY=> on electronic trading platform EBS, its lowest since Oct. 31.   \par \par \uc2  By 0355 GMT, it was buying 116.05 yen, down 4.4 percent from a 32-month peak around 121.40 yen hit earlier this month.   \par \par \uc2  The euro was at 138.70 yen <EURJPY=>, down 3.4 percent from a record high around 143.60 yen hit at the start of the week.   \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to 138.20 yen <EURJPY=R>, down nearly one yen from the level in late U.S. trade and the lowest in a month.   \par \par \uc2  The euro slipped to $1.1950 <EUR=> from around $1.1980.   \par \par \uc2  As yen-carry trades were often done with the Australian and New Zealand dollars, the backlash was also the most dramatic against them.   \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar fell as far as around 86.00 yen <AUDJPY=R>, down nearly one yen on the day and 5.4 percent lower than an 8-1/2-year peak around 91.30 yen hit earlier this month.   \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand dollar tumbled to a two-month low around 79.40 yen <NZDJPY=R>, shedding 8.3 percent from its 8-1/2-year high hit only last week.   \par \par \uc2  Some traders suspect that the sharp fall in the price of gold <XAU=> might also reflect unwinding of yen-funded investment by speculators.   \par \par \uc2  HOLIDAYS IN MIND "People are selling to take profits in assets they had bought this year," said Seiya Nakajima, chief economist at Itochu Corp. "That's what people do ahead of the Christmas holidays, so that they have a nice holiday."   \par \par \uc2  The dollar's tumble against the yen began after the Federal Reserve in its post-meeting statement on Tuesday stopped describing its policy as accommodative, sparking speculation that the Fed may wind down its 18-month tightening campaign soon.   \par \par \uc2  The spectre of rising U.S. interest rates has been the major driving force of the dollar's rally this year.   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  won a brief respite on Thursday after data showed U.S. net capital inflows to U.S. assets surged to a record $106.8 billion in October -- more than enough to cover the record trade gap in the same month.   \par \par \uc2  Analysts had expected a slowdown to around $75 billion.   \par \par \uc2  As the dollar's interest rate advantage over the yen and the euro is expected to stay for now, some traders see the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounding soon.   \par \par \uc2  "It's unlikely that the dollar will face a sharper fall from the current level," said Ibaraki at Nomura Trust. "It's likely the dollar will shuffle in a 115.50-116.50 yen range for a while."   \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan kept its policy of flooding the money market with cash when it concluded a two-day meeting on Friday.   \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui has repeated in recent months that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could end its super-easy policy. But he has met resistance from politicians about an early tightening of credit.   \par \par \uc2  Interest rates in Japan have held near zero for nearly five years while rates in other economies have risen, helping to accelerate the yen's fall this year.      \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -Yen darts to 6-week high vs dollar|LANGEN|GNN|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051216e1cg000cm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
