\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  says did not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market in Nov  \b0\par\par\uc2 304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2005\par 11:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 30 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not conduct any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between Oct. 28 and Nov. 28, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2  It is the 20th straight month that Japan has stayed out of the market, the longest stretch since the MOF began keeping such records in 1991.  \par \par \uc2  The last time Japanese authorities sold yen to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was on March 16, 2004, when it was trading at about 108.60 to the dollar <JPY=>.  \par \par \uc2  Japan sold 35 trillion yen ($292.3 billion) in the 15 months to March 2004 to weaken the yen due to concerns that excessive yen strength could weaken overseas demand for Japanese exports and hurt the nation's economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2  But the economy has since been improving gradually and Japan is widely expected to snap out of a near 8-year bout of deflation later this year or in early 2006.  \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading near a 27-month low around 119.50 yen on Wednesday, having tumbled around 18 percent since the beginning of the year.  \par \par \uc2  Yen weakness seems to be less of a concern to the Japanese government than yen strength as officials have for the most part kept quiet about the yen's fall.  \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said earlier in the month that recent yen moves largely reflected economic fundamentals, although he said the MOF would keep a close eye on market movements.  \par \par \uc2  The yen's losses have ignited market speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with a weaker yen helping Japan to overcome deflation, some analysts say the MOF is unlikely to step into the market.       \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|U|M|D|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051130e1bu000oy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
