\par\par \b \uc2 = INTERVIEW: Japan May Act If Dlr Above Y125 - Sakakibara  \b0\par\par\uc2 835 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2005\par 21:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 22, 2005 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Leika Kihara\par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Japanese monetary authorities aren't worried about the yen's recent downtrend, but may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the U.S. unit shoots up above Y125, a former top Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese investors will continue to shift money out of the nation in search of higher yields as the Bank of Japan sticks to its ultra-easy monetary policy, keeping the yen weak against the dollar at least until early next year, said Eisuke Sakakibara, a former vice finance minister for international affairs.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar is likely to climb above Y120 by the end of this year, although caution over possible dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will slow any rises from there, Sakakibara said.  \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara, who is now a professor at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Keio University, expects the MOF to verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen if it weakens to Y125 to the dollar and take direct action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if it tumbles to Y130.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm sure the MOF would get worried if the dollar heads for Y130 because such yen declines would mean Japan's economy is weak," Sakakibara said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 "If I were the MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pointman, I'd be worried enough to take action" if the dollar climbs near Y130, said Sakakibara, who was nicknamed "Mr. Yen" because of the aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigns he conducted in the late 1990s while in the government.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Group of Seven counterparts likely won't mind Tokyo conducting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem sharp yen declines, because the U.S. and euro-zone countries won't want their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise too much and hurt their economies, Sakakibara said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has gained about 18% from this year's low of Y101.67 marked in January, touching a two-year high of Y119.58 Wednesday on expectations of widening interest-rate differentials between the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 That has kept the MOF from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March 2004, when it halted a 15-month campaign to curb the yen's strength. If the MOF had stayed out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Monday, which seems almost certain, it would mark the longest period of nonintervention since such data became available in 1991.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry's absence also suggests it sees no need to act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market because Japan's economy is in good shape. In its monthly report for November issued Monday, the government said the economy is "recovering at a moderate pace," sticking to its upbeat view for the fourth straight month.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 G7 Likely To Discuss Global Monetary Tightening\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global interest-rate movements will remain the focus of attention in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and will also be the main topic when G7 finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors meet in London on Dec. 2-3, Sakakibara said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial markets around the world had been awash with excess liquidity but that's likely to change as developed nations shift toward tighter monetary policy, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rising global interest rates may prompt investors to shift money away from riskier markets and destabilize \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements in emerging Asian nations, thereby hurting economic growth there, Sakakibara said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Such change in global fund flows could happen next year, and that's likely the most pressing topic for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors" gathering in London, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The G7 meeting will be held a day after the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy meeting on Dec. 1, when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is widely expected to increase rates by 25 basis points from a historically low 2%. The Federal Reserve is also expected to raise key interest rates by a quarter point to 4.25% when its policy board meets on Dec. 13.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Japan is the one left behind in such global interest-rate hikes and that's fueling the yen's current extraordinary weakness," Sakakibara said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOJ board members have recently indicated that an end to quantitative easing, under which it guides financial institutions' current account deposits at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to a range of Y30 trillion to Y35 trillion, may come sometime early next year, citing brightening prospects for overcoming deflation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But such suggestions have raised strong opposition from government officials, including Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, who are worried that ending the ultra-easy policy hastily could hurt economic growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As if to temper such criticism, BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui has recently indicated that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will keep short-term rates near zero even if it ends quantitative easing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sakakibara said China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reform likely won't be discussed much at the London G7 meeting because Beijing isn't expected to send a delegation there.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11-22-05 0235ET  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020051122e1bm003mz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
