\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-Japan unlikely to step in way of yen's slide  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs  \par \uc2 879 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2005\par 09:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to delete extraneous line at top of text)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 16 (Reuters) - To those who think the yen's slide to a 27-month low against the dollar may prompt the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to soon \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some analysts have a simple reply: don't count on it.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation has stirred that the MOF may be fretting about the yen's broad-based slide and could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help out the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would be a stark reversal from the ministry's massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the years.  \par \par \uc2 For one thing, a weaker yen is helping Japan beat eight years of deflation and escape a "lost decade" of stagnation, along with better domestic-led growth and a rallying Tokyo stock market.  \par \par \uc2 "The current level is not so much an issue for the MOF. They still prefer a weaker yen," said Taisuke Tanaka, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 A falling yen helps the fight against deflation by boosting the cost of imported goods and putting upward pressure on prices, while also helping spur exports.  \par \par \uc2 And as Japanese officials including Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi have made clear, their worry is that the Bank of Japan could move too quickly to abandon its ultra-loose monetary policy and thereby cut short a full-fledged recovery from deflation.  \par \par \uc2 While high import costs can be negative when it comes to oil prices, so far Japanese firms seem to have little trouble coping.  \par \par \uc2 That means the yen likely has more room to weaken, at least until U.S. or Chinese officials begin to voice worries about the trade impact, some analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 President George W. Bush said on Wednesday that Japan's economic reforms are working. Bush, in Kyoto at the start of an Asia tour, did not mention the yen but is expected to pressure China to allow the yuan to appreciate when he visits Beijing.  \par \par \uc2 Only South Korea, whose exports compete with Japan's, has griped about the yen's drop to a seven-year low against the won.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts and traders believe the dollar's rise to around 119.40 yen on Tuesday means it is only a matter of time before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair overtakes the next big psychological level of 120. The dollar fetched 119.25 yen in Tokyo on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "If the U.S. and China don't express any complaints over the weaker yen, the MOF may not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if dollar/yen breaks above 125, 130 or 135," said Tanaka at Morgan Stanley.  \par \par \uc2 Already the MOF has gone 19 months without stepping into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and if it takes no action by the end of this week it will be longest stretch without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on record.  \par \par \uc2 A main reason for MOF's unprecedented 35 trillion yen ($294 billion) of dollar buying between January 2003 and March 2004 was to prevent yen appreciation from exacerbating deflation.  \par \par \uc2 FUNDAMENTALS AND VOLATILITY  \par \par \uc2 To consider \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the MOF has two standard refrains -- \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be in line with economic fundamentals and their movements not be too volatile.  \par \par \uc2 So far it has given a green light on the economic front, with Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki saying while the yen fall must be watched it is broadly in line with economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 MOF officials have also dropped mention of taking "appropriate action as needed" when asked about the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF could act if the slide turns very sharp, but the yen has had a long history of moving sharply higher, not lower.  \par \par \uc2 That is mainly due to the role of Japan's exporters in blocking the pace of any drop with their constant hedging needs to sell dollars and euros for yen.  \par \par \uc2 In fact, the dollar's rise against the yen, driven by U.S. short-term rates, has been so steady that a gauge of the pair's 10-day historical volatility is near the lowest since 1996.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF also may worry that by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  it could create more problems by igniting a powerful rally in the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar/yen selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could trigger sharper-than-intended declines, with a risk of inviting broader concerns about the dollar," Tomoko Fujii, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in Tokyo, said in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 The situation when the MOF last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars -- about 231 billion yen worth -- on June 17, 1998, at just above 144 yen, was in stark contrast with current conditions.  \par \par \uc2 At that time Japan had just suffered a severe recession and was grappling with financial panic. As a vote of confidence, the U.S. also sold dollars for yen that day and the White House praised the "bold steps" by then Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto to revive the economy.  \par \par \uc2 Reflecting how sharply dollar/yen can turn around, that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  preceded a peak above 147 yen in mid-August 1998 before it collapsed below 129 yen within a month -- helping contribute to a crisis surrounding U.S. hedge fund LTCM. Dollar/yen bottomed at around 111.50 in mid-October that year. ($1=118.94 Yen)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS) REPEAT|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051116e1bg000px\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
