\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar trims gains vs euro in profit-taking  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 851 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2005\par 02:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts; updates prices, adds comment, changes byline)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Nov 15 (Reuters) - The dollar pared some gains against the euro and yen on Tuesday in a wave of profit-taking as traders said the rally had become overextended, but cited few fundamental reasons for the reversal.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading modestly higher from late Monday as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors locked in profits on the dollar's earlier rally, which had pushed the greenback to a 27-month peak against the yen and a two-year high against the euro ahead of testimony by Ben Bernanke, the nominee for Fed chairman.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had gained modestly as Bernanke's testimony had broadly reinforced the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's view that U.S. interest rates would continue to rise, burnishing the allure of dollar deposits to foreign investors. U.S. economic data had also briefly helped support the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Bernanke's impact is to continue to affirm the market view that rates will continue to rise and the dollar is to get interest rate driven support," said Michael Jansen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at National Australia Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Bernanke vowed to fight for U.S. price stability and said he would carry on with the policies of the current Fed chief, Alan Greenspan.  \par \par \uc2 But profit taking in dollars took hold as the first installment of Bernanke's testimony ended. The testimony, which had broken for recess, was planned to resume at 3 p.m. EST (2000 GMT).  \par \par \uc2 "We're getting to levels that are really good valuations," to sell, said one portfolio manager in New York.  \par \par \uc2 A euro near $1.1650 and dollar near 119 yen are such areas, he said, noting that corporate profit repatriation flows into dollars to take advantage of a one-time 2005 tax break will favor upside for the dollar through year-end.  \par \par \uc2 But he added that people are already starting to take short U.S. dollar positions for the beginning of 2006.  \par \par \uc2 "Short" positions are effectively bets that a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will weaken.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has rallied by about 15 percent against the yen and euro in 2005, lifted by a steady series of Federal Reserve rate hikes.  \par \par \uc2 In early afternoon trading, the euro was at $1.1681 <EUR=>, slightly down from late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was at 118.99 yen <JPY=>, up about 0.2 percent. The dollar had earlier climbed as high as 119.42 yen, according to Reuters data. Traders said a major options barrier had been smashed at 119.00.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc <CHF=>, the dollar was up 0.3 percent at 1.3210.  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve's campaign since June 2004 to tighten credit, which has taken short-term interest rates to 4 percent from 1 percent, has drawn more investors to the dollar and U.S. bonds. Several economists see these overnight rates rising to at least 4.5 percent next year.  \par \par \uc2 "It looks like (Bernanke) will follow the Greenspan model in the initial period of his tenure," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. "A continued Greenspan path may be viewed as somewhat bullish, although continued Fed hikes are already priced in."  \par \par \uc2 PPI GIVES MIXED SIGNALS  \par \par \uc2 The dollar got an early boost as a stronger-than-expected rise in producer prices reinforced expectations for higher U.S. interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar surrendered some of its earlier gains as investors mulled a surprise 0.3 percent decline in October's core producer price index, stripped of volatile food and energy costs. They had expected core PPI to rise 0.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Declining auto prices accounted for the fall in core PPI.  \par \par \uc2 Producer prices, received by manufacturers, farmers and oil producers, rose 0.7 percent as surging natural gas and home heating oil costs outweighed cheaper gasoline, a government report showed. Wall Street had expected prices to be flat.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Chicago Fed President Michael Moskow said on Tuesday that more Fed rate increases are "appropriate," and that nobody can answer whether the Fed will stop at a "neutral" monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 Such hawkish comments from the Fed were in stark contrast to pointed criticism from the Japanese government this week on that country's monetary policy, comments which have prompted market players to think the Bank of Japan will be cautious about lifting rates from virtually zero, a level which has hobbled the yen this year.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar's advance against the yen has prompted talk of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  taking action.  \par \par \uc2 "On the fringes of the market, there is beginning to be some talk of the possibility that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell U.S. dollars," Marc Chandler, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Brown Brothers Harriman, said in a note to clients. "While possible, the probability seems low at the present." (Additional reporting by Nick Olivari, Kevin Plumberg, and Jamie McGeever in New York)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 8)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|J|UKI|Z|CANT|AUF|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051115e1bf0002g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\