\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Dlr/yen moves in line with fundamentals-Tanigaki  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2005\par 07:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments, background, byline)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 1 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday dollar/yen exchange rates have been in line with economic fundamentals but still needed to be closely watched as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were moving more than in the past.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki declined, however, to comment on whether he regarded recent dollar/yen moves as rapid, or to speak about specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels.  \par \par \uc2 "They are moving largely in line with economic fundamentals," Tanigaki, who was reappointed as finance minister in a cabinet reshuffle on Monday, told a group of reporters when asked about the dollar's rise to a two-year high against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 But he added: "They are moving more than in the past, so we need to carefully watch them."  \par \par \uc2 By late afternoon in Tokyo the dollar was around 116.50 yen <JPY=> after having surged through suspected option barriers near that level to 116.68 yen on electronic trading system EBS earlier in the day, its highest since September 2003.  \par \par \uc2 Asked what was meant when Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) would "take appropriate action as needed" at times of yen weakness, Tanigaki said: "That is a delicate question ... I cannot clearly comment on that."  \par \par \uc2 Financial players usually interpret the phrase to mean the MOF is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.  \par \par \uc2 But ministry officials have recently dropped the reference to taking "appropriate action as needed" when asked about the yen's decline. A similar statement by Vice Finance Minister Koichi Hosokawa in mid-October prompted some speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen's fall.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF said on Monday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from Sept. 29 to Oct. 27, marking the 19th consecutive month that Japan had stayed out of the market.  \par \par \uc2 The last time the MOF conducted yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on March 16 last year, when the dollar fell nearly two yen on the day to around 108.60 yen <JPY=>.  \par \par \uc2 But the last time the ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop a fall in the yen was in June 1998, when it dropped to around 146 yen to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Lately, officials have been reiterating that the ministry would carefully monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements and that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  needed to reflect economic fundamentals and move stably.  \par \par \uc2 BOJ POLICY SPECULATION  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki was also cautious when asked about growing talk on how and when the Bank of Japan would end its ultra-easy monetary policy, repeating that Japan's economy was still in mild deflation.  \par \par \uc2 In a twice-yearly outlook report on Monday, the BOJ raised its forecasts for prices and economic growth and said chances of a policy shift would grow over the course of the fiscal year starting next April.  \par \par \uc2 "I think it is good to have various discussions. But what we are interested in most is what is happening now. Mild deflation remains and we need to continue making efforts to overcome it."  \par \par \uc2 Japan's economy has been plagued by more than seven years of deflation. But with consumer prices expected to start rising in coming months, financial markets believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could abolish the four-year-old "quantitative easing" framework of flooding the banking system with funds as early as the first half of next year.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-JAPAN-TANIGAKI-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|U|M|D|NAT|ABX|BNX|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051101e1b1000e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
