\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Japan Jawbones Again, This Time To Help Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,143 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2005\par 06:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 By Laurence Norman  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par   NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--It will come as no great surprise to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  watchers to hear that Japanese officials are again practicing one of their most polished arts: jawboning the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par   After months of silence on the yen from senior officials, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's top bureaucrat said overnight that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate 'should basically move stably reflecting economic fundamentals.' \par   What might surprise investors, however, is the cause of the renewed verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Whereas in the past couple of years, Japanese jawboning has been aimed at stemming yen strength, Vice Finance Minister Koichi Hosokawa's remarks Thursday seemed an attempt to curtail the yen's hefty recent declines. \par   Given that a weaker yen should help Japan's exporters and bolster the economy, Hosokawa's remarks beg some basic questions: what exactly are the Japanese worried about, could verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  be followed by action and - most importantly - will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's officials be as successful in stemming yen losses as they were in curtailing its gains? \par   Of all the major economies, Japan's officials have the reputation as the most effective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  jawboners. Euro zone squealing about single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength last year was undermined by the mixed message from officials, with European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials showing no enthusiasm for stepping into markets while member state finance ministers demanded action. \par   The U.S.'s strong dollar policy mantra fell on deaf ears as the greenback dropped to multiyear lows. However, Japanese comments seemed carefully coordinated to maintain constant uncertainty about if and when the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , discouraging traders from testing key levels. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was a real threat too: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spent trillions of yen buying dollars in late 2003 and early 2004. The language grew more animated as the dollar declined toward the Y100 mark at the turn of the year, a level it never fell through. \par   In the same tradition, Hosakawa's comments - as befitting an opening gambit - were measured. He told reporters that he would not comment on daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements, but then went on to make the remark about yen moves needing to reflect fundamentals. That was the same warning Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki gave a year ago to warn the market from pushing the yen too high. \par   However, there was an extra bite to Hosakawa's words.   He said Japanese monetary authorities are ready to take action in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market 'if necessary,' but didn't comment on what kind of actions he had in mind. \par   Bank of Japan Executive Director Masaaki Shirakawa also said Thursday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is closely watching \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate movements. \par    Why Now? \par   On one level, the timing of the comments is odd. Most analysts agree that the weaker yen could provide the final piece in the country's economic recovery from years of anemic growth and deflation. \par   They also say a weaker yen makes life easier for the Bank of Japan as it looks to gradually tighten its ultra-easy monetary policy. After all, with the yen far below what most Japanese companies and bank strategists expected in early 2005, a pickup in yen demand once the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  starts tightening policy would be unlikely to propel the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to levels that authorities will feel anxious about. \par   The dollar lifted to a fresh 25-month high against the yen Wednesday at Y115.98 and is up 6.7% in the last six weeks. It was at Y115.57 late Thursday in New York. \par   However, Monica Fan, global head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at RBC Capital Markets in London, said the sliding yen does present causes for concern. \par   The main reason is that Japanese officials - and they are not alone in this - lay a stress on orderly, stable financial markets as crucial underpinnings for the domestic and global economies. Stability is all the more valuable as Japan's recovery is still a work in progress and as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prepares for a new era in its policy stance. \par   'The last thing the Bank of Japan wants at this moment is volatility,' Fan said. \par   Fan said that if the yen continues to weaken, the positive impact on exporters could be outweighed by the 'reduced confidence in asset markets.' After all, if yen weakness wipes out the gains made by Japanese stocks in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  terms, foreign investment in the Nikkei will rapidly decline. \par    Rates Still Dollar's Ace \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fan said that for now, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to keep its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purely verbal. However, if the dollar's rise takes it near Y120, it is not inconceivable authorities would step in. 'I would never rule it out,' she said. 'Y120 is a psychologically important level where investors...will become concerned about the scope for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .' Meanwhile, Japanese officials must hope their words will soothe market movements. There are no guarantees. The yen certainly has many things going for it, but this has been true for months, and yet the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has continued to slide. The economy is improving, Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi won huge backing for his reform agenda in September's election, while pressure for China to allow the yuan to strengthen should aid the yen. There was also evidence Thursday in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weekly portfolio data that the massive capital outflows from Japanese purchases of foreign bonds was beginning to ebb. Brian Rose, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at the Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi in New York, said that with short-term speculative investors - like hedge funds - continuing to hold extreme bets against the yen and the U.S. still running a yawning current account gap, 'we could definitely see a sharp pullback' at some point. However, as Rose noted, the large and growing spread between U.S. and Japanese interest rates 'is working so hard against the yen, it's hard for the other factors to upset it.' That was also the view of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists at Citigroup. In a research note Thursday, the analysts said the yen would remain under pressure 'until there is a strong signal by the bank that deflation has ended and that a (monetary) policy change is imminent.' 'We do not believe that such a change will occur before the middle of 2006,' the note added. The bank recommended taking a short position in the yen versus the dollar, targeting a dollar move up to Y118.50. If that view proves popular, Japanese officials may soon need to up their rhetoric. (Laurence Norman covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. He has previously covered the Argentine economy for Dow Jones in Buenos Aires.) -Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires, 1-201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com (Azam Ahmed in New York and Lieka Kihara in Tokyo contributed to this article) -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020051021e1al0028l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
