\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Yen near 116, and slide may not be over yet  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs  \par \uc2 870 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2005\par 11:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 19 (Reuters) - At the Dubai meeting of the Group of Seven industrial powers two years ago, it all seemed so simple: fix the world's troubling trade imbalances by getting the Japanese and Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate.  \par \par \uc2 As the market seized on the theme of dollar weakness helping to rein in the U.S. current account gap, the dollar quickly crashed below 115 yen and sank as low as 101.65 in January this year.  \par \par \uc2 Several events since then should have sent the yen even higher. The U.S. deficits have only worsened, China finally unshackled the yuan's peg to the dollar, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  abandoned its unprecedented dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  19 months ago.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, the yen has been plagued by short-term interest rates at virtually zero, incessant Japanese demand for higher-yielding overseas bonds, China's ultra-cautious reform of the yuan and more foreign investors hedging the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk of their hefty Tokyo stock purchases.  \par \par \uc2 With dollar at two-year highs solidly back above 115 yen some analysts say 120 yen is a real possibility despite Japan's recovery from 15 years of stagnation.  \par \par \uc2 What could improve the yen's fortunes, analysts say, would be a halt to the Federal Reserve's 15-month credit-tightening campaign that has driven the dollar's broad rally, along with any evidence of a slowdown in the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 For now, though, Fed officials have made clear their worries about inflation worsening and their confidence that the economy will expand solidly next year, indicating that rates will keep rising.  \par \par \uc2 If nothing else, the dollar's rally this year and big buying of U.S. securities by foreigners, despite the mammoth deficits, have underscored the appeal of higher yields. By contrast, the Bank of Japan is likely to tread cautiously in ditching its ultra-loose "quantitative easing" policy, preventing much of a rise in still-meagre bond yields for disgruntled domestic investors.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ will also likely leave sort-term rates low enough for investors to feel comfortable continuing to sell the yen to buy other high-yielding assets in the "carry trade".  \par \par \uc2 "The move out of quantitative easing is something new for the BOJ, and they're going to do it so gradually that the yen will be an attractive funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for some time," said Sharada Selvanathan, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 Investors even think the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is poised to raise rates in 2006 for the first time in more than two years.  \par \par \uc2 Speculators have all but given up on a change in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy helping the yen, after having been frustrated by the small July revaluation of the yuan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tight grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese households, burned from the Nikkei stock average's crash in the 1990s followed by a plunge in property prices, are gradually regaining confidence to make riskier financial bets -- but by ploughing cash into foreign bonds.  \par \par \uc2 So strong has the demand been for foreign bonds that it has more than overwhelmed the voracious hunger for Tokyo shares from overseas investors playing the Japan recovery story.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign bond buying by Japanese investors has totaled a whopping 12 trillion yen ($104 billion) so far this year, outpacing the 8.67 trillion of Tokyo shares snapped up by overseas accounts.  \par \par \uc2 THE POWER OF HEDGING  \par \par \uc2 More importantly, the role of hedging seems to be playing a key role in the yen's slide.  \par \par \uc2 Because the difference between short-term rates in Japan and the United States is the biggest since 2001 and still widening, it has become very pricey for big Japanese investors to hedge their dollar bond buying against any U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness.  \par \par \uc2 In interviews with Reuters, some of Japan's top life insurers have said they are ditching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedges or buying foreign bonds on a unhedged basis.  \par \par \uc2 Combined with the big demand from households for foreign bond mutual funds and investment trusts that buy foreign bonds on an unhedged basis, the flood of fleeing capital has swamped the yen.  \par \par \uc2 At the same time, foreigners who have stuffed money into Japanese shares expecting an extra fillip from any yen gains may rethink that strategy and hedge more of their purchases, especially since yen hedging costs almost nothing.  \par \par \uc2 Stephen Jen, Morgan Stanley's chief global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, has warned such a change in hedging could have major consequences as the U.S. fed funds rate, now at 3.75 percent, heads even higher. He sees dollar/yen in a 115 yen to 120 yen zone.  \par \par \uc2 "The big risk is there will be a powerful wave of hedging by foreign investors, and a reduction in the hedge ratio of Japanese investors," he said in a research note this week.  \par \par \uc2 With some economists seeing the fed funds rate rising as high as 5 percent next year, let alone Japan's favoured New Zealand dollar offering 6.75 percent and likely heading higher as well, yield-starved Japan is having no trouble abandoning the yen.  \par \par \uc2 ($1=115.79 Yen)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -YEN (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051019e1aj000ow\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
