\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Sep Foreign Reserves Fall By $4.2 Billion  \b0\par\par\uc2 433 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2005\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell $4.2 billion in September from August to $843.56 billion, the first decline in two months, as the value of the country's U.S. bond holdings dropped, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The decrease in the reserves, which includes convertible foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gold and International Monetary Fund special drawing rights, followed a $8.39 billion gain in August and a $4.16 billion fall in July.  \par \par \uc2 According to International Monetary Fund data, combined \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of China and Hong Kong in June overtook those of Japan for the first time on record, reaching $837.9 billion and making them the world's biggest foreign reserves holder, a finance ministry official told a press briefing.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's reserves stood at $834.0 billion in that month, the MOF report based on the IMF data said.  \par \par \uc2 But compared with reserves held by mainland China alone in June, which stood at $715.9 billion, Japan in July remained the holder of the world's largest foreign reserves for the 70th straight month, the MOF report showed.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's reserves totaled $830.1 billion in July, the latest month for which the IMF data are available, according to the MOF report. China's reserves data for July are not yet available from the IMF.  \par \par \uc2 Data from the IMF differ slightly from those issued by the MOF due to differences in methods used to calculate the value of gold.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF official said that gains in U.S. Treasury bond yields decreased the value of Japan's government bond holdings in September, leading to the fall in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in that month. The bulk of Japan's foreign reserves are thought to be held in Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of September, the 10-year Treasury yield was 4.328%, up from 4.017% at the end of August, the official said.  \par \par \uc2 The euro's decline against the dollar in September also reduced the value of Japan's euro-denominated assets, he said.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was quoted at $1.2026 in New York at the end of that month, down from $1.2346 at the end of August, according to the MOF data.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have shown only minor changes corresponding to asset price and exchange rate fluctuations since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign in March of last year.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Shawn Schroter \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 07-10-05 0200GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051007e1a70003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
