\par\par \b \uc2 Japan seeks gentle G7 stance on yuan, wary of oil  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 876 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2005\par 10:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 20 (Reuters) - Japan goes into a Group of Seven (G7) finance chiefs' meeting later this week in remarkably good economic shape -- at least by its standards over the last decade.  \par \par \uc2 As satisfied as Japanese authorities may be about present conditions, though, they know sustained growth depends on a united front with their G7 partners to deal with high oil prices and gently persuading China to press on with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform.  \par \par \uc2 Just when Japan's economy is showing signs that it is at last firing on all cylinders, including domestic consumption that had been depressed by years of deflation, sky-high oil prices threaten to derail the global economic expansion.  \par \par \uc2 And although the volatile yen, a headache for Japan's exporters for most of the last three decades, has been steady, China's yuan reform pose tricky questions on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials say the G7 will reaffirm its solidarity on those issues but, when it comes to a communique, the group will keep any changes to the minimum and be diplomatic on the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 "No one has any major problem with how the dollar, euro and yen have been moving against each other, so there is no big issue there,"said Eisuke Sakakibara, a former top financial diplomat known as "Mr. Yen" after engineering a G7-led reverse of the soaring yen in 1995.  \par \par \uc2 "Because of political pressures at home, the United States may make some noises, but I don't think there will be any new statement on the yuan," he told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 He added that there was little that finance ministers and central bankers from the G7 -- the United States, Japan, Germany, France, Britain, Canada and Italy -- could do about oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 YUAN REFORM  \par \par \uc2 Friday's G7 meeting will be the first since China dropped the yuan's decade-old peg to the dollar in favour of a managed float system on July 21.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 quickly hailed the move, but a tiny 2.1 percent revaluation against the dollar at the time and the yuan's minute rise since have angered China critics, notably U.S. lawmakers who say an undervalued yuan gives China an unfair trade advantage.  \par \par \uc2 Crafting a G7 communique to satisfy everyone is therefore a daunting task, given the market's sensitivity to any changes.  \par \par \uc2 "We can change the wording every time, but that could invite speculation. On the other hand, if we repeat the same language over and over, it becomes stale," said a top \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official who declined to be identified.  \par \par \uc2 When a call for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility was introduced in the communique after a meeting in Dubai two years ago, the market took it as being pointed at the yen as well as the yuan, driving up the value of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dismay of officials in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Zembei Mizoguchi -- a successor to Sakakibara who as Japan's G7 "sherpa" negotiated to counterbalance "flexibility" with a passage that said excessive volatility was undesirable -- said the fine balance of recent communiques should stay.  \par \par \uc2 "On \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the G7 has already established a common platform at their February 2004 meeting in Boca Raton," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi can be forgiven for his attachment to the statement. Partly because of the fallout from the Dubai flap, he ended up orchestrating \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record 35 trillion yen ($313 billion) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign in the 15 months to March 2004.  \par \par \uc2 BITTER EXPERIENCE  \par \par \uc2 Japan's desire to avoid pressuring China too much also comes from its own bitter experience -- the yen's surge after the Plaza Accord, struck 20 years ago by the then Group of Five.  \par \par \uc2 The G5 agreed to coordinate monetary policies and jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the dollar in a bid to rein in the bulging U.S. trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to around 150 yen from 240 within a month of the Plaza pact, eventually bottoming near 80 yen in 1995. It was trading around 111.75 yen on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki, at a news conference this week, recalled how the yen's export-crimping rise hit the economy and how an easy monetary policy fed a "bubble" in property and stock prices that ultimately crashed in the early 1990s.  \par \par \uc2 He added that attempts to support the economy with fiscal spending in the 1990s left Japan with debt totalling 150 percent of its gross domestic product, the worst in the industrial world.  \par \par \uc2 "Directly linking the Plaza Accord with this (debt) may seem a bit hasty...but I do think there is some connection with the problems we face today," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 The good news on fiscal reform is that a resounding election victory by Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi earlier in the month has given additional impetus for potentially painful reform, something Tanigaki can boast about to his G7 counterparts.  \par \par \uc2 This comes on top of decent economic growth figures -- annualised 3.3 percent in the April-June quarter after a 5.8 percent expansion in January-March -- and rapid improvement in the health of the banking sector.  \par \par \uc2 GROUP-JAPAN|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|R