\par\par \b \uc2 IMF Expects Moderate Japan Economic Growth In Near Term  \b0\par\par\uc2 512 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2005\par 05:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The International Monetary Fund says it expects moderate economic growth in Japan in the near term, but that low trend growth and population aging pose risks to the country's longer-term prospects.  \par \par \uc2 "The financial system has been stabilized, the corporate sector is more resilient, labor markets have become more dynamic, and fiscal consolidation is under way," the IMF said in its annual Article IV Consultation report. "As a result, the economy appears better positioned to sustain an expansion."  \par \par \uc2 It projected that Japan's real gross domestic product would grow by 1.8% in 2005 compared with 2.6% growth in 2004, while it predicts private consumption will increase 1.2% this year compared with 1.5% last year.  \par \par \uc2 But the IMF said that Japan's economy faces risks in the medium term.  \par \par \uc2 "Population aging is pushing up social spending, and modest underlying growth leaves the economy susceptible to shocks," it said in the report, dated July 26 and released Monday.  \par \par \uc2 To respond to such risks, Japan needs to make further efforts to "advance fiscal and structural reforms in order to strengthen the underpinnings for more robust self-sustained growth," the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's fiscal deficit remains large, with the general government deficit estimated to be 6.9% of GDP in the fiscal year ended March, while the public debt is set to rise further, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 IMF directors "encouraged the authorities to save any potential tax windfall and to avoid any expansionary measures in this year's supplementary budget, assuming the expansion continues as envisaged," the report said.  \par \par \uc2 The directors also saw scope for further streamlining public investment and for civil service reform, as well as comprehensive tax reforms, including an increase in the consumption tax, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 On monetary policy, the IMF said it agreed with the Bank of Japan that keeping the account balance target at its present level was appropriate for now to show that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is committed to maintaining its easy policy.  \par \par \uc2 It said that clear communication with markets on the BOJ's policy intentions will be key as deflation ebbs.  \par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, a few of the IMF directors that consulted with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  felt that market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be warranted as a last resort if a stronger yen seriously threatens to drag down prices or derail the economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 However, most other directors "pointed to the economy's improved ability to handle a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and cautioned that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should only be used under extraordinary circumstances," the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 The report, completed late last month, also said the IMF welcomed the planned privatization of Japan's postal system, because that "could help level the playing field for private financial institutions."  \par \par \uc2 Monday, the Upper House of Parliament voted down Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's legislation to privatize the postal system, making the future of the project unclear.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Kenneth McCallum, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; ken.mccallum@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson [ 09-08-05 0326GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050809e1890004u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\pi