\par\par \b \uc2 DATA VIEW:Japan Leading Indicator Hints At Bright Future  \b0\par\par\uc2 562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2005\par 11:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A survey of key economic indicators Friday suggested Japan's economy will continue improving, adding to recent signs that better domestic demand is supporting the world's second largest economy.  \par \par \uc2 The index of leading indicators rose to 60 in June, the first time for it to rise above the so-called boom-or-bust line of 50 in five months, Cabinet Office data showed.  \par \par \uc2 A reading above 50 for the index, which looks at 12 economic indicators that predict future economic developments, points to expansion over the coming six months while a level below 50 suggests contraction.  \par \par \uc2 Ten of those leading indicators were available, and six of them improved, the data showed.  \par \par \uc2 The survey also showed that the coincident indicators index, measuring the current state of the economy, had climbed to 100. Nine of the 11 indicators that make up the survey were available, and all of them showed improvements, the government said.  \par \par \uc2 The positive leading indicators survey comes as speculation mounts as to when the government will declare that Japan's economy is ready to begin to recover solidly on increased corporate capital spending and recovering exports, after staying in a lull in recent months.  \par \par \uc2 Asked about whether Tokyo might say as early as next week that the economy has emerged from its sluggish period, economic minister Heizo Takenaka said: "We would like to make clear our judgment (on the issue) in the monthly report we'll issue" Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Other data released Friday indicated that private consumption, which accounts for more than a half of Japan's gross domestic product, hasn't recovered completely.  \par \par \uc2 Overall household spending fell 0.1% on year in June after being adjusted for price changes, the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications announced.  \par \par \uc2 That marks the third straight month of declines following a 2.0% fall in May and a 3.0% drop in April. Private consumption for the April-June period also declined 1.7% on quarter in real terms after slipping for the two previous quarters, the data showed.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the lackluster consumption figures, however, analysts said the outlook for the Japanese economy remains positive.  \par \par \uc2 "I think we can put some faith in the leading index figure," said Koji Fukaya, chief macroeconomic researcher at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. "With exports gradually recovering and capital investments driving growth, the economy's prospects aren't bad."  \par \par \uc2 Recent steady improvements in income and employment suggest that consumption will pick up in coming months, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Separately, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell $3.88 billion in July from June to $839.66 billion, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday, as rising yields depressed the value of Japan's government bond holdings.  \par \par \uc2 The fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which include convertible foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gold and International Monetary Fund special drawing rights, follows a $1.07 billion rise in June and a $1.13 billion decline in May.  \par \par \uc2 Since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign in March last year, its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have shown only minor changes due to asset price and exchange rate fluctuations.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's reserves are the biggest in the world, with China coming second.  \par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Kenneth McCallum and Shawn Schroter [ 05-08-05 0944GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050805e185000ex\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
