\par\par \b \uc2 Fund transfer in Japan budget won't affect yen-MOF  \b0\par\par\uc2 365 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2005\par 10:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 4 (Reuters) - A planned transfer of surplus funds from a special account holding Japan's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets to the budget's general account will have no impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Some market participants have speculated that the transfer, which usually takes place in August if the budget's special account yields a surplus, could push up the yen this month.  \par \par \uc2 But the official said the market had misunderstood the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the transfer did not involve any selling of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for yen.  \par \par \uc2 Takao Hattori, chief global strategist at UFJ Tsubasa Securities, said the yen tends to rise in August on various factors, including market talk that Japanese authorities may need to sell foreign assets and buy the yen to complete the transfer between two accounts in the budget.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a jinx that the yen tends to rise in August and this is one of the things that give rise to it," Hattori said.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry official said it has to issue financing bills (FBs) to obtain yen funds to complete the transfer, rather than selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the special account for yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 "We issue FBs and do not sell foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for yen in the market. There should be absolutely no impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from the transfer," the official told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 He added that although the transfer usually takes place in August, the ministry has already procured funds for it by issuing FBs.  \par \par \uc2 This year, surplus funds totalling 1.419 trillion yen ($12.77 billion) will be transferred to the general account from the special account.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has the largest foreign reserves in the world, standing at $843.537 billion at the end of June. The reserves ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a record 20 trillion yen in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since then.  \par \par \uc2 ($1=111.15 Yen)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-JAPAN-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -TRANSFER|LANGEN|J|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050804e184000qq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
