\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Ex-MOF Mizoguchi: Expanding  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Band 1 China Option  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 931 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2005\par 05:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--China may move further toward \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility "not so far in the future" as it's likely to face renewed global pressure to slash its huge dollar reserves, a former top bureaucrat at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 To do that, Beijing may expand the yuan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band or adjust more flexibly the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's central parity, said Zembei Mizoguchi, a former vice finance minister for international affairs.  \par \par \uc2 Strong domestic opposition toward change could prevent Chinese authorities from taking further steps in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform this year, but nevertheless Beijing may not be able to wait too long, Mizoguchi said.  \par \par \uc2 "Sooner or later, China will be forced to make a very tough decision" on whether to free up the yuan further, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing's decision on July 21 to raise the yuan's value against the dollar by about 2%, a smaller margin than expected, was a "realistic way" to balance U.S. pressure for urgent action and strong domestic opposition against taking any steps that may slow economic growth, Mizoguchi said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 "By acting promptly, China showed it was playing its role as a major actor in the world economy," Mizoguchi said. "But the fact that it revalued the yuan by a small margin shows that opposition (within China) toward a large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revaluation was actually quite strong," he said.  \par \par \uc2 China said it was moving away from its tightly managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg keeping the yuan at about CNY 8.277 to the dollar. Chinese monetary authorities set the new value of the yuan at CNY8.11 to the dollar and said the yuan's exchange rate will now be set in reference to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi, who served as Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrat from January 2003 to June 2004 and oversaw a massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign by Tokyo, is currently president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Center for International Finance.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par China May Not Act In 2005 But Can't Wait For Too Long \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Surging Chinese exports to other countries will continue to flood the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market with excess dollars, which Beijing would need to absorb through market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  even under the new system, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In shifting to the new system, China has explained it has adopted a managed floating rate system that's based more on market supply and demand," Mizoguchi said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But if it keeps absorbing excess dollars through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and doesn't allow the yuan to move much from CNY8.11 to the dollar, people may start to doubt whether China has indeed moved toward \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flexibility," after which they will "start asking Beijing to do more," Mizoguchi said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beijing may either decide to expand the present 0.3% \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band against the dollar or allow the yuan's central parity to gradually move up to better reflect growing demand for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has countered speculation that its latest move would be the first of many revaluations to come, with the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  saying that exchange-rate policy adjustments are aimed at gradually adjusting the regime, not necessarily the level of the yuan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, People's Bank of China Governor Wu Xiaoling was quoted in a recent interview as saying that market forces will determine the value of the yuan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Referencing a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is different from pegging (the yuan) to a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Wu said in an interview published in the Shanghai Securities News. "In this case (of referencing), formation of the yuan exchange rate needs to use market demand and supply as an important base," he was quoted as saying.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Dollar Likely To Keep Rising \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mizoguchi said any further change in China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  system likely won't much affect moves in major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , such as the dollar/yen and euro/dollar, because the issue is "one of many factors" influencing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He also played down the view that the yen and euro may rise against the dollar if they are included in the basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  China uses as a reference in guiding its new exchange-rate policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Chinese authorities will use the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket as a reference, not as something to peg the yuan to," because pegging the yuan to a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be too complicated and "won't be \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " for them, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mizoguchi said it was "natural" for the dollar to bounce back against the yen after tumbling briefly on news of China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  revaluation because "what determines the moves of major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are economic fundamentals and interest-rate differentials."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many investors who had been betting on yen rises against the dollar are starting to unwind their yen-bullish positions, which will also continue to support the U.S. unit ahead, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After falling about two yen to Y109.87 just after China's announcement, the dollar quickly bounced back above Y112. At 0330 GMT Friday, it was changing hands at Y112.20, up a bit from Y112.12 late Thursday in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the U.S. economy performing better than that of Japan and Europe and U.S. interest rates on the rise, the dollar is expected to continue rising "for some time," Mizoguchi said. "Everyone wants to invest in a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that has high liquidity and generates higher yields," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson [ 29-07-05 0334GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050729e17t00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
