\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  flexibility to make Asia less  \highlight22\uc2 interventionist \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 14:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 22 (Reuters) - A rally in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on speculation of more China revaluation raises the threat of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but official tolerance of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength is likely to grow over time as exchange rates become more flexible. China abandoned its decade-old dollar peg and revalued the yuan by 2.1 percent to 8.11 per dollar from 8.28 on Thursday, adopting a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage the yuan. Malaysia followed suit and moved to a managed float.  \par \par \uc2 The smaller-than-expected move is seen as Beijing's first step towards greater exchange rate flexibility, and offshore forward contracts imply that investors expect the yuan to rise at least 6 percent in a year's time.  \par \par \uc2 Following China's move the yen, the most liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region, made its biggest one-day gain in more than three years and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged against the dollar, prompting monetary authorities to fire a warning shot about volatility.  \par \par \uc2 "In the medium to long term there would be more revaluations and the authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth the move. But the tolerance level the authorities are comfortable with has risen -- this is a relative competitiveness argument," said Adarsh Sinha, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital.  \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility has been a sensitive topic in the market after Group of Seven rich nations urged Asia to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise to help correct global imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 But these export-oriented Asian countries have been reluctant to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation which would damage their competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 LESS \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTIONIST\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 One-month dollar/yen volatility, a gauge of how volatile the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair would be in the future, rose to its highest level since April of 8.9 percent as the spot rate fell sharply. It trimmed gains on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, U.S. Treasury debt prices plunged and benchmark 10-year yields hit a two-month high as investors worried that China's move would curtail demand for U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 "The China move has encouraged speculation that the Asian authorities will becomes less \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the future and Treasury yields rose promptly," UBS said in a note to clients.  \par \par \uc2 During the dollar's three-year fall up to 2004, Asian central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent export-damaging strength in their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , spent more than $300 billion in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 5 quarters to the end of March 2004. Other Asian countries, which either peg their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar or impose strict \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, also conducted heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But short-term, their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to continue. There were already signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Singapore on Thursday, and China and Malaysia on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan ended down at 8.1111 per dollar on Friday, suggesting some action from the authorities.  \par \par \uc2 "Clearly the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been heavily in the market... Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers have the tradition of regulating the market with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and they are unlikely to give up just because of China's revaluation," said Thomas Stolper, global market economist at Goldman Sachs.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no doubt the Japanese will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at some stage if they get an impression the yen strengthens too quickly and too much and the same applies to other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Japan, Singapore and South Korea have repeatedly warned they would take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary.  \par \par \uc2 "For (emerging Asia), I also believe \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a major threat. To some of these central banks, their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have already appreciated against the dollar in the last 3-1/2 years," said Stephen Jen, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Morgan Stanley.  \par \par \uc2 "If the yuan starts to appreciate, it would only be 'catching up' to them, and not a justification for them to appreciate further."  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050722e17m00156\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
