\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-End to dollar peg in Asia to fuel  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserve shift  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 870 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 2005\par 18:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 21 (Reuters) - Increasingly flexible Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  following China and Malaysia's moves to abandon dollar pegs on Thursday could prompt the region's central banks to diversify further away from the dollar, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 China revalued the yuan by 2.1 percent and adopted a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , allowing it to appreciate to 8.11 per dollar from the previous 8.28. Malaysia also dropped its 7-year-old dollar peg in favour of a managed float.  \par \par \uc2 There were no details on the new basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  the yuan is pegged against, but analysts say China could buy fewer dollars and re-adjust its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves -- the world's largest after Japan at above $700 billion.  \par \par \uc2 "This China move, especially given the anticipation that this might be replicated in other countries in the region, I imagine that investors will now assume the stock of dollar reserves Asian central banks hold will be reduced," said Michael Metcalfe, senior strategist at State Street.  \par \par \uc2 Diversification by central banks worldwide, who have nearly $4 trillion of reserves, is a sensitive topic for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 In the three years to 2004, the dollar lost 30 percent against the euro, due partly to expectations central banks in emerging countries are shifting away from the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the dollar lost more than two yen to a three-week low of 110.16 yen <JPY=> after China's move. Against the euro however, the fall was limited and it traded near $1.2130 <EUR=>.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of New York said the dollar and yen might have equal effective weightings in the basket of around 40 percent each with the euro at 20 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Citigroup said in a note to clients: "Today's move is a conclusive signal that the yuan likely will become progressively less linked to the dollar over time."  \par \par \uc2 "Given our expectations for China to lead a broader move by Asia economies away from their dollar-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this implies that foreign official purchases of U.S. assets eventually will decline."  \par \par \uc2 DIVERSIFICATION EVIDENCE  \par \par \uc2 There has already been some evidence that foreign central banks are buying less U.S. Treasuries this year.  \par \par \uc2 HBOS Treasury Services estimates in the first 5 months of this year, U.S. Treasury asset holdings by the 25 largest emerging countries including China rose by $79 billion, compared with a gain of $113 billion in the same period last year.  \par \par \uc2 At the same time, these countries' combined \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose by $175 billion in 2005, compared with the increase of $145 billion last year.  \par \par \uc2 HBOS estimates these central banks sold more than $1 billion a day against other developed market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the fourth quarter of last year. It also said China already has 40 percent of reserves in non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a recent shift.  \par \par \uc2 "In the first 4 months of this year reserve diversification is ongoing. As the exchange rate becomes more flexible, China will start managing euro/yuan or yen/yuan more actively as part of the basket and accumulate these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the course of its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the dollar," Pearson said.  \par \par \uc2 HBOS estimates the euro's share in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of the 25 emerging countries has risen to 30 percent from 25 percent in the past two years.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank for International Settlements gives the dollar's share in global reserves at 69 percent in 2004 while the euro's share was 21 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Pearson said the net effect from Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility can actually be dollar positive as Asian central banks accumulate less reserves.  \par \par \uc2 "Reserve accumulation in Asia is bad for the dollar against G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and good for the dollar against developing countries. This is totally the opposite of what is required to rebalance the global trade," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "But the move to greater flexibility in China is going to start to correct that imbalance. Eventually it means regional central banks accumulate less reserves in dollars and they will have less dollars for sell for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Even with the evidence of diversification, the dollar gained around 10 percent against the euro since January.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  THREATS  \par \par \uc2 During the dollar's three-year fall, Asian central banks have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent export-damaging strength in their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which spent more than $300 billion in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the five quarters through the end of March 2004, has already fired a warning shot after China's move, saying it would take action if needed.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers reported Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Thursday to smooth the appreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after it surged 2 percent against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Short-term, if there is the evidence of disorderly moves, central banks would like to come in and smooth the move," Metcalfe said.  \par \par \uc2 "Looking further ahead, China has revalued now, in theory it should increase all the Asian authorities' tolerance for domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength against the U.S. dollar."  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2 -RESERVES (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|Z|NAT|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050721e17l0026w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
