\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ INTERVIEW:Focus On Yield Gap To Push Dollar To Y115 - MS  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 787 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2005\par 14:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar will likely rise to Y115 by the end of September as market players focus more on the widening yield differential between Japan and the U.S., a top economist at Morgan Stanley said Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ascent to such a level would force Japanese investors, many of whom are still expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken due to the huge U.S. current account deficit, to reconsider their views and start buying dollars in earnest, said Stephen Jen, Morgan Stanley's chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist.  \par \par \uc2 'Investors are starting to reconsider their thesis (for a weak dollar) but aren't convinced yet that their thesis is wrong,' so in that sense 'the market is way ahead of investors,' Jen said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 'Because investors are chasing after the market, when the dollar goes to Y115, they'll run' to buy dollars, thereby pushing it even higher, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Jen said he 'won't be surprised' if the dollar goes into the Y115-Y120 zone in the third quarter of this year, a move that would be 'completely off the radar screen of Japanese investors.'  \par \par \uc2 The dollar touched a fresh 11-month high of Y112.35 in Asia Thursday, gaining around 5.5% from its June low of Y106.51 as market players increasingly turned their attention to the growing interest-rate gap between Japan and the U.S. At 0618 GMT, the U.S. unit was at Y112.25, up from Y112.18 late Wednesday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Jen believes this trend will continue because the global market has shifted its focus to bond-market themes from equity-market ones. When the dollar was tumbling to a near five-year low of Y101.67 in January, inflows of foreign capital into Japan's equity market played a powerful role, he said.  \par \par \uc2 But now, 'the key is the Bank of Japan versus the Federal Reserve,' which bodes ill for the yen, Jen said. That's because the BOJ likely won't start tightening monetary policy any time soon, while the Fed is on course to hike the federal funds target rate close to 4%, he said. Downside Risks For Dollar  \par \par \uc2 To be sure, there are some downside risks for the dollar, such as the chance of the yen getting pushed up as a result of a shift in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime later this year, Jen said.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing keeps the yuan in a tiny 0.3% range above or below CNY8.2770 to the dollar. The U.S. has been pushing China to free up the yuan, arguing that China is keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - and thus the price of the nation's exports - artificially low, hurting U.S. manufacturers and taking American jobs. China has long said its ultimate goal is to liberalize its yuan policy, but officials have said outside pressure won't cause them to hurry to make changes.  \par \par \uc2 Some market players argue that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the yen, could rise if the yuan moves up as a result of Beijing's loosening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg as such an increase would make exports from Asian nations competitive relative to Chinese goods.  \par \par \uc2 Jen said there's a 60% chance of China shifting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime by September, given last week's decision by U.S. Senators Charles Schumer and Lindsey Graham to postpone until autumn a vote on legislation that would impose a 27.5% tariff on Chinese imports if Beijing fails to revalue the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 That move was 'very, very smart' because it has 'aligned' the voting date to 'other pressure points' for China, such as the Group of Seven finance leaders' meeting in autumn and a scheduled release around October of the U.S. Treasury Department's semi-annual report, which could officially name China as guilty of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation if it doesn't act by then, Jen said.  \par \par \uc2 'If China doesn't move by September, it will not move this year,' he said. But even if Beijing does act, the dollar will likely hold above Y108 due to the market's focus on Japan-U.S. yield differential and the threat of yen-selling, dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stayed out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March last year, but MOF officials have suggested the ministry could take action if the yen makes excessive or speculative rises following a change in China's exchange-rate system. -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Kenneth McCallum  \par \par \uc2 -0-  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050707e17700810\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
