\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-G8 keeps pressure on Asia to adopt flexible  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Sabyasachi Mitra and Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2005\par 18:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds analyst quote)  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 11 (Reuters) - Finance ministers from the world's rich nations on Saturday urged emerging Asia to adopt flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies even though they stuck to tradition and did not mention \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their final communique.  \par \par \uc2 The ministers from Group of Seven industrialised nations plus Russia (G8) used a breakfast meeting with their counterparts from Brazil, Russia, India, China and South Africa to keep up pressure on developing Asian economies to adopt market-based exchange rates. "The convention is that when G7 meetings happen with finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors for general purposes we include a statement on exchange rates," British Finance Minister Gordon Brown told a news conference.  \par \par \uc2 "It's also a convention that when a G7 and G8 prepares for a summit, there is no statement on exchange rates," Brown said, when asked whether the absence of exchange rates from the communique meant the pressure for more flexibility had abated.  \par \par \uc2 In a separate statement, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow called for increased exchange rate flexibility in emerging Asian economies to redress global economic imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 "Vigorous structural reforms are needed in Europe and Japan to lay the foundation for vibrant growth, as well as increased rate flexibility in Asia," Snow said.  \par \par \uc2 Snow on Friday met Chinese Finance Minister Jin Renqing.  \par \par \uc2 "We had a very good meeting with the Chinese," Snow told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 Washington has been pressing China to revalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, arguing it keeps the yuan artificially low and gives Chinese exports and unfair advantage.  \par \par \uc2 Last month, the United States issued an ultimatum to China, saying that if Beijing failed to let the yuan appreciate by October it faced the possibility of being named named a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator, which in turn could invite trade sanctions.  \par \par \uc2 Europe has also complained that the yuan, or renminbi, pegged in a narrow band around 8.28 per dollar, is undervalued.  \par \par \uc2 China has long said that it plans eventually to give market forces a greater role in determining the yuan's value but it has stood firm against international pressure and has said any change would be shaped primarily by domestic factors.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN'S GENTLE APPROACH  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told a news conference that China needed to adopt flexible exchange rates in the not so distant future but it was up to Beijing to decide on the timing and means to reform its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.  \par \par \uc2 "On yuan, basically this issue when, how and what they should do, it's the responsibility of the Chinese government," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 Although Japan thinks China should make its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime more flexible in the near future, Tokyo has been less vocal as it believes that putting external pressure on Beijing could actually delay the process of adjusting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 "The important country here in the yuan debate is Japan as it has sided with China and is allowing China to move at its own pace," said Mansoor Mohi-Uddin, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has tried to dampen market speculation saying it did not think the yen would appreciate if China allows the yuan to rise.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market if the yen appreciates when China moves. But within the G7, the United States and Europe are unlikely to tolerate massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  like those seen in 2003 and 2004," Mohi-Uddin said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 2003 and until March 2004 to curb the export-damaging rise in the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The New York Times reported on Friday that the Politburo was considering steering the yuan's value in relation to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead of just the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But Canadian Finance Minister Ralph Goodale told Reuters the idea of linking the yuan to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a sign of willingness to give some ground but would not go far enough.  \par \par \uc2 A basket system would let the yuan trade more flexibly, but the initial exchange rate could be very close to the current peg.  \par \par \uc2 GROUP-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050611e16b000aw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
