\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ Morgan Stanley Ups Yr-End Dlr Forecast; Latest To Revise  \b0\par\par\uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2005\par 03:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 By Laurence Norman  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par   NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Morgan Stanley became the latest large \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer to revise up its dollar forecasts Thursday, taking a more bullish tone on the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  both in the near-term and for year's end. \par \par   The dollar's recent rally has forced the euro down to a more than eight month low and lifted the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to multi-month highs versus the pound and the Swiss franc. \par \par   Last week, a host of banks revised upward their dollar forecasts amid heavy losses by the euro following the 'no' votes in the French and Dutch referendums on the European Union constitution. \par \par   In a research report late Thursday, Stephen Jen, Morgan Stanley's London-based head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research, said the bank now sees the euro at $1.23 at the end of June, $1.20 on Sept 30 and $1.19 at the end of the year. He predicted a euro rate of $1.18 in 12 months. The bank previously saw the euro at $1.28 on Sept 30 and at $1.26 at year's end. The euro was at $1.2220 late Thursday. \par \par   Jen said the bank sees the dollar at Y105 on June 30, at Y101 at the end of the third and fourth quarters and at Y102 in 12 months. Previously, the bank forecast the yen at Y98 at the end of the third quarter and at year's end. The dollar was at Y107.44 late Thursday. \par \par   Jen said the revised view is partly based on his belief that the U.S. will be able to withstand negative effects of its 'super-sized' current account deficit for longer, because that deficit is a reflection of low U.S. savings but also of excess savings abroad. \par \par   'This means global imbalances will likely reach levels much higher than most people think possible; and the  (dollar) will remain resilient for much longer than is consistent with traditional trade models,' he wrote. \par \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The current economic and political troubles for the euro zone economies will focus the market back on the region's fundamental problems and weigh on the euro, which Jen believes became 21% over-valued at its record high in December. Pressure on the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to ease monetary conditions could also send the euro lower. However, unlike many analysts who are bearish on the dollar in the medium-term but more positive for 2006, Jen said the dollar could come under pressure again late next year 'if current trends in relative growth and the U.S. current account deficit continue.' For the yen, Jen said that bigger and growing interest rate differentials between Japan and the U.S. and the possibility of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the dollar falls toward Y100, would weigh on the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In addition, Jen believes the chances of a significant revaluation of the yuan are falling. 'As a result, while I still believe 'something': will happen in the (the second half) this year, the risk of China doing nothing this year has risen somewhat,' he wrote. Earlier Thursday, Goldman Sachs also upped its year-end dollar forecast. It sees the euro at $1.30 at year's end from $1.35 previously. Last week, the bank sharply raised it near-term dollar predictions, while a host of other large \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading banks also revised their dollar forecasts up. The median forecast of a Dow Jones Newswires survey of 19 leading \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers last week found they expect the euro to trade at $1.22 in one month, $1.25 in three months and $1.30 at the end of the year. -By Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050610e16a000v5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
