\par\par \b \uc2 REPEAT: Dodge: Canada Overnight Rates To Rise Over Time  \b0\par\par\uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2005\par 03:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge said Thursday that Beijing should move as soon as it can to a free-floating exchange rate policy.  \par \par \uc2 He added that he was not in favor of a mere revaluation of the yuan, which China currently keeps tightly pegged to the U.S. dollar, as such a move would not be in the interests of either China or the global economy.  \par \par \uc2 Dodge was speaking at the Canadian Chamber of Commerce in Tokyo. [ 09-06-05 0120GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 "We think China should move as quickly as possible to a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Dodge said.  \par \par \uc2 "We are not arguing that they should revalue, because a modest revaluation is not in China's or the world economy's interests," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Dodge also reiterated the Bank of Canada's long-standing view that floating exchange rates best allow an economy to adjust to external shocks.  \par \par \uc2 Dodge said that when an economy has a fixed exchange rate, wages and prices can't respond flexibly to changes in the global economy, leading to shrinking output, rising unemployment or very high inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Financial authorities have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to keep an exchange rate fixed. If this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "sterilized," as is the case in China, then this also temporarily prevents movements in wages and prices, Dodge said.  \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sterilizes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by issuing short-term debt instruments to soak up the liquidity it added to the money supply through its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In the past, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prevent the yen from appreciating. However, it's unreasonable to compare \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to China because Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't sterilized, he said in response to reporters' questions.  \par \par \uc2 China also faces the challenges of raising consumption in the household sector and reforming its financial system so that excess household savings can flow into new loans, Dodge said.  \par \par \uc2 Dodge also expressed frustration with debate on financial reform in China but was hopeful that there would be progress in the future.  \par \par \uc2 "We, as central bankers, are disappointed that we haven't had sufficient, hard-nosed and difficult discussions with the Chinese about these issues," Dodge said. "We've skated around these issues but haven't confronted them head on."  \par \par \uc2 "At G20 in Beijing in October, hopefully we can work a little harder on these problems," he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par          Challenges For Global Economy And Japan \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dodge said that while it's important to address global economic imbalances, it's equally important to avoid labeling a country with a current account surplus as "good" and one with a deficit as "bad." The current account is an economy's broadest measure of trade in goods and services with the rest of the world.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dodge added that a country's ratio of outstanding debt to gross domestic product should remain at sustainable levels but stopped short of elaborating further.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He also said that Japan's fiscal deficit problem is unsustainable, and it's up to Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to take the necessary steps.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dodge acknowledged that consumer prices in Japan have responded poorly despite the Bank of Japan's ultra-easy monetary policy that keeps short-term interest rates at zero and floods the financial system with excess liquidity.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he said Japan isn't the only country where prices behave irregularly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even though we've been pressing against (full) capacity in a number of countries - Australia, Canada, the U.K. and the U.S. - we haven't generated the levels of inflation that we saw in the 60s, 70s or 80s," Dodge said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Something has been happening to traditional relationships that we don't fully understand," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dodge repeated the Bank of Canada's view that interest rates will have to rise "over time." The Bank of Canada has left its trend-setting overnight rate unchanged at 2.5% at its last five policy meetings. Its next meeting is July 12.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He also said he expects inflation in Canada to be near its target by the end of 2006. Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  currently aims to keep on-year rises in the core consumer price index at the midpoint of a 1.0% to 3.0% target band. This inflation target is up for review in 2006.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Stanley White, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; stanley.white@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson and Shawn Schroter [ 09-06-05 0255GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050609e1690002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
