\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Ex-MOF Utsumi: US Likely To Raise Yuan Issue At G8  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 953 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2005\par 07:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Top U.S. policy-makers might stress at a Group of Eight finance ministers' meeting next month that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to be stronger, a former top Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S.'s mention of China's yuan policy at the G8 leading nations' meeting in London would show how strong its irritation at Beijing has become because the meeting is expected to focus on development issues, and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't otherwise likely to be a major topic.  \par \par \uc2 However, "the U.S. has been hardening its rhetoric over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and therefore, there's a good chance for the Chinese yuan to become a topic of discussion" at the G8 meeting on June 10-11, Makoto Utsumi told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "But I don't think (the G8 ministers) will tackle \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues head-on" because \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs won't attend the meeting, said Utsumi, who served as vice finance minister for international affairs between 1989 and 1991.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the finance ministers are also unlikely to mention China in a statement they will issue after the meeting, which is a preparation for the G8 summit of state heads scheduled for July 6-8.  \par \par \uc2 Utsumi's remarks come as U.S. President George W. Bush's administration becomes more vocal in urging China to revalue the yuan, in the face of increased pressure on it do so by U.S. senators lobbying for manufacturers.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said Thursday, for example, that China must let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate against the dollar significantly.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    China Can't Resist Pressure Forever \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beijing has promised to reform its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime that keeps the yuan pegged to around CNY8.28 to the dollar, but it has insisted it will proceed with the reforms at its own pace, so it isn't seen as bowing to foreign pressure.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But "China is probably considering the period between June to autumn as a possible timing" to start loosening the peg, said Utsumi, currently president of Japan Credit Rating Agency Ltd.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the U.S. Congress threatening high tariffs on China-made goods, "I don't think China can stick for long to its stance that it will resist any external pressure," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Those bills aimed at China, including the Schumer bill, will come up for formal debate (at the U.S. Congress) by July."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Schumer bill, proposed by Sen. Charles Schumer, D-N.Y., and Lindsey Graham, R-S.C., calls for a 27.5% tariff on all the goods China ships to the U.S.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say the bill is unlikely to become law as it violates World Trade Organization rules. But U.S. Congressmen have given it wide bipartisan support as they run out of patience with the Bush administration's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  diplomacy with China.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If Beijing keeps digging in its heels, "the U.S. government will be forced to take some specific measures against China," Utsumi said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By holding off from revaluing the yuan, China also risks prodding the U.S. Treasury Department to label Beijing a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator in its twice-a-year report, whose next installment is due in October, Utsumi said. Analysts say that would be a step toward trade sanctions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    China May Link Yuan To Basket Of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 In changing its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime, as a first step, "China will probably take the yuan off the dollar-peg and then link it to a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , thereby letting its trading range gradually widen," Utsumi said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I believe that China has already laid the technical groundwork needed" for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy change, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Utsumi added that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to curb any "excessive" falls in the dollar against the yen that some traders say might occur if China lets the yuan rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rumors of an imminent yuan revaluation tend to spark speculative buying of Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the view that they should move with the yuan because they're all Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The idea that a more flexible yuan should lead to a stronger yen is erroneous, baseless and incredible," Utsumi said. "It's impossible to reach that conclusion whatever mathematical calculations you employ."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If (Tokyo) conducts \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with an aim to foil volatile movements stemming from such untenable speculation, it can fully justify its action" even to those U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities who accuse China of artificially keeping the yuan weak, Utsumi said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ways China manages its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves suggest the yen should weaken instead if the yuan is unpegged, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "To maintain the yuan's peg, China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been buying considerable sums of dollars" that enter the country's financial system in exchange for the yuan, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But that's not the end of the story. China, as part of its diversification strategy, has been converting a good portion of those dollars into yen and euros. That means that the current peg system is supporting the yen and euro against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Utsumi said he doesn't know the actual breakdown of China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  portfolio. But the pace of Chinese buying of U.S. Treasury bonds - Beijing's preferred vehicle for carrying its dollars - has been slower than the pace of its foreign reserves buildup, suggesting China has been shifting into other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's no doubt they're doing it."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Kenneth McCallum and Hugh Lawson [ 27-05-05 0534GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050527e15r0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
