\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW:US Protectionism Will Deter Japan MOF -Sojitz   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2005\par 06:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)-- Japan will likely keep a low profile in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets even if the yen resurges against the dollar so it can avoid becoming a target of U.S. protectionism like China, said an expert in U.S.-Japan economic diplomacy.   \par \par \uc2 In the past few months, U.S. lawmakers have increased threats of trade sanctions against China, accusing Beijing of keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, artificially and unfairly cheap to sell more Chinese goods in the U.S.   \par \par \uc2 Under such circumstances, the last thing Tokyo wants to do is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prevent the yen from strengthening, as that would prompt U.S. protectionists to shift their focus to Japan, Tatsuhiko Yoshizaki, chief economist and executive vice president of Sojitz Research Institute, told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.   \par \par \uc2 "If (\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers) move now, they could be in big trouble," Yoshizaki said. "They would risk being included in Schumer's anti-China bill."   \par \par \uc2 Yoshizaki was referring to a bill proposed by Sen. Charles Schumer, D-N.Y., and Lindsey Graham, R-S.C., calling for a 27.5% tariff on all goods China ships to the U.S. Although it violates World Trade Organization rules, the bill has managed so far to garner wide bipartisan support in Congress on the back of rising anti-China sentiment.   \par \par \uc2 "So far, the focus of the U.S. Congress has fallen on China instead of Japan," Yoshizaki said, pointing out import limits the U.S. has recently put on Chinese garments.   \par \par \uc2 But Yoshizaki noted that the seeds of complaints against Japan are gradually taking root in the U.S.   \par \par \uc2 "Americans now perceive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as nothing but an evil," he said.   \par \par \uc2 In addition, the strong performance of Japanese auto makers in the U.S. at the expense of struggling General Motors Corp. (GM) and Ford Motor Co. (F) has already made Tokyo vulnerable, Yoshizaki added.   \par \par \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp. (7203.TO) and other Japanese car makers have become increasingly concerned about a replay of the trade tensions that surfaced in the late 1980s and early 1990s.   \par \par \uc2 In 1995, for example, the world's top two economies came close to a trade war as Washington threatened high tariffs on Japanese vehicle exports to pry open Tokyo's auto and auto-parts market, which U.S. manufacturers accused of being closed to foreigners.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Hard For \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  To Justify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. pressure has been gradually building on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The Financial Times reported that 47 members of Congress earlier this month sent President George W. Bush a letter calling on the administration to cite China and Japan as nations that are manipulating their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to verbally \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market" to curb yen strength, they said in the letter.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With protectionism regaining momentum among U.S. lawmakers, "Don't ever expect the U.S. to buy the argument that Japan is contributing to the stability of U.S. interest rates by buying Treasury bonds" with the dollars it purchased through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Yoshizaki said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's experiences show that it doesn't matter to the U.S. whether the logic of your arguments is correct" once Congress cements its view that "you're unfair," he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the MOF hasn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for more than a year, its officials have maintained they would act if the yen rises excessively against the dollar in order to defend the economy. MOF officials also say the Group of Seven industrialized nations agree with that stance.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But watching U.S. auto makers struggle while seeing Japanese rivals perform strongly, U.S. citizens "will in no way listen" to the MOF's argument, Yoshizaki said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nor is George W. Bush likely to extend a helping hand to Tokyo, Yoshizaki said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The fact that protectionism is resurfacing reveals that the Bush administration is losing control over Congress," he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To Tokyo's delight, the dollar has recently been stable around Y107.50, well above this year's low of around Y101.67 marked in January, but some major players like JPMorgan Chase Bank still expect it will dip to a decade-low of Y100.00 in the coming months.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Takeshi Takeuchi and Chris Gallagher [ 26-05-05 0429GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050526e15q0003a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscal