\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Yen Should Gain If Yuan Revalued - Dai-Ichi  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2005\par 10:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese officials say there is no reason why the yen should rise against the dollar if China revalues its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but a senior economist at a major Japanese life insurer says they've got it all wrong.  \par \par \uc2 As long as Beijing lets the yuan rise only gradually against the dollar, say by 3% to 5% this year and then by the same margin again in 2006, Japan will see more economic benefits than harm, said Takashi Kadokura, a senior economist at Dai-Ichi Life Research Institute.  \par \par \uc2 "First of all, a gradual climb in the yuan is unlikely to cause a slowdown in Chinese domestic demand," Kadokura told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview. In fact, "that'll boost the purchasing power of Chinese consumers, and China-bound exports from Japan would likely increase," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese manufacturers relying on sales within China - such as firms producing cosmetics, clothing and wristwatches in particular - will see their profits grow, Kadokura said.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing has for years kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegged around CNY8.28 to the dollar. But there is growing international pressure on the Chinese to loosen that peg and let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise to reflect the strength of China's economy.  \par \par \uc2 So far Beijing has played down talk of it revaluing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon, but Kadokura expects to see a change this year as the Chinese government may need to prevent a pickup in inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Some U.S. lawmakers and manufacturers have insisted that the yuan is undervalued by as much as 40%, which acts to subsidize Chinese exports.  \par \par \uc2 The size of undervaluation is around 18% according to Kadokura's calculation. But a one-time revaluation of such a magnitude is very unlikely as it would harm the Chinese economy, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Kadokura also noted that a gentle yuan rise would make it difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb any yen rise in tandem with the revalued yuan.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Net Effect Of A Stronger Yuan Positive For Japan \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese officials say Japan's exports will get no boost from a stronger yuan as the two countries don't compete much in the same export products. Therefore, the yen shouldn't rise, they say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kadokura disagrees.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Increased buying power among Chinese consumers means that they'll begin to spend more on higher-quality products," Kadokura said. "The quality of Japan-made products is very high, so their sales will grow more quickly in China" than those made by U.S., European and other manufacturers, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stronger Chinese buying would offer a much-needed shot in the arm for Japan's sluggish economy. According to Japanese Finance Ministry data, increased exports to China accounted for 44% of the total rise in Japan's exports over the last three years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, some analysts say a stronger yuan could have adverse effects on Japan. It will raise output costs in China for Japanese manufacturers such as apparel and electronic-machinery makers. These firms have shifted production there to take advantage of the low-wage labor market and then export their goods from the mainland to the U.S. and elsewhere.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, Kadokura said the likely growth in sales within China would keep the net effect of mild yuan gains positive for Japan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Data from Japan's trade ministry showed that China's domestic consumption accounted for 43% of the sales of products produced by Japanese companies in the mainland in the first nine months of last fiscal year ended March 31, up from 35% for the full fiscal year 2003. Analysts expect that ratio to keep climbing as China's economy expands.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Bubbling Chinese Property Market Poses Risk \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The major risk to Kadokura's yen-positive scenario is the red-hot property market in China, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's this idea that if you invest in Chinese real estate, you'll be able to pocket good amounts of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  profits if the yuan is revalued," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thus, if the initial yuan rise is small, "speculative money may start flowing into China's property market at a faster pace, fueled by the expectation that more yuan revaluations will come," Kadokura said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That's troubling for China because such hot-money inflows could result in the bursting of its property-price bubble and then an economic downturn, Kadokura said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If that happens, Japan's economy would then suffer from weakened Chinese demand for exports, thereby making the yen less attractive for investors, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That would be more damaging to Japan (than to the U.S.) as it depends more on China in terms of exports."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson [ 19-05-05 0819GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050519e15j000fq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgo