\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  did not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market in April-MOF.  \b0\par\par\uc2 178 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2005\par 12:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 28 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said on Thursday it had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in April, suggesting authorities were not overly concerned with the yen's recent exchange levels against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 MOF data showed the ministry stayed away from the market from March 30 to April 26, as expected. The ministry has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since mid-March last year.  \par \par \uc2 It sold a record 20 trillion yen ($189 billion) in calendar 2003 and another 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004, fearing that excessive yen gains could hurt the Japanese economy. The sharp falls in the dollar that the ministry was trying to prevent around that time have subsided. Since March 2004, the dollar has traded in a range between 101.67 yen and 114.90 yen.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the dollar was trading at 105.9 yen , down from its six-month peak of 108.90 yen hit earlier this month. ($1=105.87 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050428e14s008hx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
