\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ INTERVIEW: Immediate Yuan Shift Unlikely-Ex-MOF Mizoguchi  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Miyako Takebe Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2005\par 08:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--China may widen the yuan's current trading band as a first step in shifting to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, but is unlikely to do so immediately as conditions are not yet right, a former top bureaucrat at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 'Chinese officials are aware of the need to alter its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system and are likely proceeding with preparations,' Zembei Mizoguchi, a former vice finance minister for international affairs said.  \par \par \uc2 But for officials to make the final decision on starting the liberalization process, 'they will likely need more concrete evidence of rising inflation' or 'see signs that (not moving) could trigger a crisis,' Mizoguchi told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 Such signs would likely appear when speculative capital inflows into China grow so large that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can no longer absorb the excess cash in the banking system - or sterilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted to keep the yuan within its current wafer-thin trading band against the dollar - in order to control inflation, Mizoguchi said.  \par \par \uc2 But 'there seems to be no such signs yet,' the former Japanese ministry official said, suggesting that Chinese officials are unlikely to reach a decision to alter its current system in the immediate future.  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi declined to speculate, however, on when such a decision may come, saying that the timing is something that is only known to Chinese officials.  \par \par \uc2 China currently keeps the yuan effectively pegged to the dollar, allowing it to trade within a thin band of 0.3% either side of 8.277 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  then absorbs excess banking-system cash to reduce the likelihood that capital inflows will spark problems like runaway money-supply growth or inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing is under growing pressure to shift to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, especially from the U.S., where textile makers claim the de-facto dollar-yuan peg is giving China an unfair advantage in global markets.  \par \par \uc2 'Fixing the reminbi (yuan) to the dollar is beginning to significantly work to the detriment of the Chinese economy,' U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan told the Senate Budget Committee last week. 'The sooner they move off this fix, the better off it'll be for China's economy,' he said.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan's unusually direct comments followed similar tough talk from U.S. President George W. Bush and Treasury Secretary John Snow who over the past two weeks have said China should make changes 'now.'  \par \par \uc2 But China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor insisted over the weekend that domestic priorities will determine the pace of its reforms. 'We are making some preparations,' Zhou Xiaochuan said Saturday, referring to reforms to the domestic banking system. But 'it's a gradual process...We have our own sequencing of reforms according to the Chinese situation.'  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi said the decision to start moving to a more flexible exchange rate regime would require extreme political consideration, as once the process is in motion, it will likely lead to continued efforts to keep pushing ahead with liberalization.  \par \par \uc2 Even if the band is widened just slightly initially, speculative capital inflows are unlikely to abate, and may result in the need for the band to keep widening, Mizoguchi said.  \par \par \uc2 Ultimately, Chinese officials may be looking to adopt a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket system with a set trading band, Mizoguchi said, but it will likely first have to decide how to make the initial entry into the liberalization process.  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi also said that the impact from any yuan revaluation on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market would be relative to movements in other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs, such as dollar/yen or euro/dollar.  \par \par \uc2 While many in the market expect a stronger yuan to lead to a stronger yen, Mizoguchi said that might not necessarily be the case if, for example, the yuan absorbs some of the market's need to sell dollars. Instead of the dollar falling against the euro or the yen, it might fall against the yuan, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi, who served as Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrat from January 2003 to June 2004 and oversaw Tokyo's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign, is currently president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Center for International Finance. -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Iain McDonald  \par \par \uc2 -0-  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050427e14r003xp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
