\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD:Don't Bet On A Solid Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Bid  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Agnes T. Crane  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,020 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2005\par 20:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--China's apparent willingness to speed up the loosening of its controversial \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the dollar sparked speculation Monday about what this would mean for the Asian nation's appetite for Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 But some analysts point out that bond investors may be missing the bigger point: Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand as a whole for Treasurys is already a shadow of its former self.  \par \par \uc2 In recent months, private investors have replaced with gusto official institutions as the driver behind foreign flows into Treasurys. And this class of market participants differs from steady, reliable central banks in a significant way: their investing decisions are driven by price.  \par \par \uc2 "It's passing underneath the radar because the numbers are still pretty big, but they are private flows, not public flows. There's been a real shift in who is buying now," said William Prophet, interest rate strategist at UBS in Stamford, Conn.  \par \par \uc2 "The significance of going from official to private flows is private flows tend to be capricious," he added. Foreign central banks tend to be less sensitive to the relative value of a security and more keen to channel funds into safer-haven securities no matter what the price.  \par \par \uc2 Recent data from the Treasury Department showed that private investors accounted for $31.5 billion, or 74% of the $42.5 billion of Treasurys bought by foreigners in February, the largest amount recorded since July 2003.  \par \par \uc2 Historically, private funds have dominated cross-border flows, but the surge in demand from central banks, particularly from Asia in 2003 and 2004, overwhelmed their significance and fueled the belief that low long-term interest rates were the direct result of official foreign buying. Yields on the 10-year Treasury market are still low, but the reasons have become less clear-cut now that one important bulwark looks less steady.  \par \par \uc2 "This year is likely to see significantly less buying of U.S. Treasuries - and U.S. fixed income in general - by Asian central banks than in any of the past three years," said Goldman Sachs economists in a recent research report.  \par \par \uc2 The economists estimate that the big four Asian central banks - those of Japan, China, Korea and Taiwan - will buy less than $100 billion of Treasurys in 2005. While this is hardly chump change, it would represent the smallest amount for this group since 2001 and about half of that bought in the previous year.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's one-year plus hiatus from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets goes a long way in explaining why Asian official buying of Treasurys is on the decline. In 2004, Japan alone snapped up $160 billion of Treasury bills and notes as it recycled the dollars bought in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the yen from appreciating. A stronger yen threatened to derail the export-led economic recovery at the time.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Looking To The Bottom Line \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  no longer \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and its overall holdings of Treasurys on the decline since August, investors have looked to other Asian central banks to make up for the shortfall.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China, with its burgeoning foreign reserves, at first blush looked like the natural successor to the Bank of Japan. By keeping its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the yuan, pegged to the dollar and enforcing strict capital controls, the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is awash in dollars. Its foreign reserves have swelled to $659.1 billion at the end of March, a 50% increase from a year earlier. Though China keeps the composition of its holdings a closely guarded secret, the bulk of the reserves are believed to be in dollars.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So when People's Bank of China Governor Zhou Xiaochuan surprised international financial markets over the weekend by saying that pressure from other nations could help accelerate the transition from a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg toward a more flexible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime, U.S. bond market participants took notice.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Typically, bond investors have viewed such a potential move as negative for U.S. government securities since it would most likely result in a diminished need for dollars and by extension Treasurys, where many of those bucks eventually find a home. A revaluation could engender a knock-on effect in the region. An appreciation of the yuan would also enable other Asian nations to allow their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to rise against the dollar, lessening the need to accumulate dollars and subsequently Treasurys.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But China is no Japan. Whereas the island-nation preferred investing in Treasurys, particularly shorter-dated ones like bills, analysts believe that China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has greater appetite for risk and has shown a clear preference for securities that give its bottom line a bigger bang for the buck.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The shift in reserve accumulation away from Japan and towards China may thus prove supportive of agencies relative to Treasuries," Goldman Sachs economists wrote.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prophet of UBS notes that China bought approximately $5 billion of agencies - which include mortgage-backed securities - in February and only $1.3 billion of Treasurys. Compare this with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heyday when it purchased $30.6 billion Treasurys in February 2004. "No one is saying they aren't buying dollar assets, they just aren't buying Treasurys.," said Prophet.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At least they aren't buying them in the aggregate. Prophet and analysts from Banc of America Securities have their eye on what appears to be China's willingness to take advantage of higher yields in the long end of the Treasury curve.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While China bought little more than $1 billion of Treasurys total in February, they bought more than $9 billion of long-dated notes. This means they were net sellers of bills, implying that China is also not the steady, conservative bank that the Bank of Japan has proven to be in the past. Like many private investors, China is going long on long-dated securities to squeeze out some extra incremental yield.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122; \par agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 04-25-05 1406ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 4083  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020050425e14p00022\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
