\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan Tanigaki says sees no change in G7  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  view.  \b0\par\par\uc2 408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2005\par 03:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 12 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday he saw no change in views on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  among the Group of Seven industrialised countries when they meet this weekend.  \par \par \uc2 The G7, at its last meeting in February, reiterated a year-long appeal for less volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and greater flexibility in exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 "I do not think there will be discussions that will change the present stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Tanigaki told a news conference.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki is set to attend a meeting of G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs in Washington this weekend on the sidelines of the regular meetings of the World Bank and International Monetary Fund.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates have been a hot focus of G7 meetings in the past, but discussion on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen low-key this time given recent calm in the market.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar is well off a five-year low of 101.67 yen hit in January and was trading at around 107.63 in Tuesday trade.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts also say talks on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies may also be limited due to China's absence from the meeting and the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has refrained from conducting yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for over a year.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki signalled that the G7 talks may instead focus on the recent rise in oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 "There could be discussion on how rising oil prices are having an effect on the global economy as a whole," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "We are closely monitoring moves in oil prices."  \par \par \uc2 While Japan's relatively efficient use of energy makes it less vulnerable to oil shocks compared with other economies, its dependency on exports leaves any economic recovery at risk to possible slowdowns caused by higher oil costs in the United States or China.  \par \par \uc2 Japan depends almost entirely on imports for its oil, but consumes only half as much oil as the United States on a per capita basis and even less relative to the size of its economy.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan has built an economy that is the most resilient to oil price rises among industrialised economies," Tanigaki said, citing Japanese efforts to cut its dependency on oil and boost energy efficiency after an oil price surge in the 1970s.  \par \par \uc2 "What we fear the most is for regions that are less resilient to oil prices to be hit and cause trouble for the global economy," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050412e14c00231\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
