\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan denies changing  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  mix in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 845 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2005\par 09:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 4 (Reuters) - Japan on Monday sought to quash speculation that it might sell dollars as part of a strategy to diversify the world's biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, saying such an "irresponsible" move would disrupt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 Playing down persistent talk Japan may reduce the exposure of its $841 billion reserves to the dollar's weakness, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it wanted to improve returns in each \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  held but had no plans to change the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mix of its reserves.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability is the first and foremost objective, and that's why we conduct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with them in the first place," a senior MOF official told reporters. "It's within that framework that we are seeking to improve returns."  \par \par \uc2 "Our reserves are so big that if were to act irresponsibly there would be a big disruption in the market," he said in a briefing on the thinking behind Tokyo's reserves management.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have been on alert for any sign of changes in the reserve policies of Japan, China, Taiwan and South Korea, which are the world's top holders of external reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Together, the four hold nearly $2 trillion after their market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem export-crimping rises in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and any remarks suggesting a shift in their reserves could have a big impact on exchange rates and global trade.  \par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's comments last month that diversification in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves was necessary sparked a wave of dollar selling as the comments were interpreted as implying a reduction in dollar holdings.  \par \par \uc2 But the MOF official said Koizumi was not talking about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  make-up of the reserves.  \par \par \uc2 "Prime Minister Koizumi never spoke about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  composition. He just said diversification was in general a good thing and that we also need to take into account such things as safety and returns," the official said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan does not disclose the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  make-up of its reserves but most of the funds are believed to be in U.S. dollars because their build-up over the years has largely been a result of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 TREASURIES A STAPLE  \par \par \uc2 A strong yen hurts the competitiveness of Japanese exports, the main driver of the country's economic recovery, and reduces overseas earnings of companies when funds are repatriated.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent a record 20 trillion yen ($186 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004 as the dollar fell more than 20 percent against the yen from its peak to bottom in that 15-month period.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has since refrained from making further market forays amid criticism from its trade partners that it was giving undue advantage to its exporters and exposing taxpayers to big losses should the dollar's weakness erode the value of reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF has estimated that unrealised losses in the reserves due to the yen's strength could reach 11.4 trillion yen ($106 billion) by March 2006.  \par \par \uc2 But this is cushioned by the handsome interest income from foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets that compare with virtually zero interest paid on finance bills that the MOF issues to fund \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Asked if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should limit the size of its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves if Japanese rates were to rise or foreign investment returns fell, the MOF official reiterated that that would not be the case.  \par \par \uc2 He said the law clearly stated that the purpose of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Special Account, of which the reserves are a part, was exchange rate stability and this objective had priority over profits or losses of the account.  \par \par \uc2 "Also, since the mid-1960s, the spread between Japan's short-term rates and 10-year U.S. Treasuries has never inverted. The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  account has been one of the profitable ones for Japan," the official said.  \par \par \uc2 The emphasis on the funds' role in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  meant that safety and liquidity had the priority over returns in the MOF's asset allocation, he added.  \par \par \uc2 "When time comes for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (to defend the yen), we must be able to liquidate a large amount of these investments instantly, and the U.S. Treasury market is the best place to do this. That's why we need to keep investing a certain amount of funds in them," the official said.  \par \par \uc2 Still, U.S. agency bonds and other securities will play an increasingly important part of the portfolio in order to optimise the overall returns, he added. He declined to disclose how much of those securities would be held as part of Japan's reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF also said it would also buy and sell securities more flexibly on the secondary market, rather than just buying them at auctions and holding them until maturity in a so-called buy-and-hold strategy.  \par \par \uc2 It said too that it would pay close attention to duration, the sensitivity of a portfolio to interest rate changes. ($1=107.71 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050404e1440038s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
