\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan MOF - to use caution in managing  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \b0\par\par\uc2 219 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2005\par 04:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 4 (Reuters) - Japan stressed on Monday that it would use the utmost caution in managing the world's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, while pursuing maximum profitability within safety and liquidity restrictions.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which controls Japan's $840.6 billion in external reserves, said in a statement that it would give the highest consideration to avoiding distortions to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other financial markets.  \par \par \uc2 The statement comes amid criticism that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record 35 trillion yen ($325 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the 18 months to March 2004 to rein in a strong yen had boosted reserves to unwieldy levels.  \par \par \uc2 A strong yen hurts the competitiveness of Japanese exports, the main driver of Japan's economic recovery, and reduces overseas earnings of companies when funds are repatriated.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants have been on alert for any sign of changes in reserve policies of Japan, China, Taiwan and South Korea, which are the world's top holders of external reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The four countries have a combined total of nearly $2 trillion after buying dollars in the market through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem export-crimping rises in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Any shift in their reserves could have a big impact on exchange rates and global trade.   \par \par \u